{"_type":"item","id":"019f817b0f0e7b9882aece4b37c893a9","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"PCB plugin: components need a polarity / orientation indicator (pin-1 / polarity mark) for correct placement","created_at":"2026-07-20T21:42:33","updated_at":"2026-07-20T22:34:24","priority":2,"tags":["component-model","orientation","pcb","placement","plugin","polarity"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019fa9624b5a7f9ca70c089d921f7675","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"No-worktree gate runs race with concurrent sibling agents on the same checkout","created_at":"2026-07-28T15:40:19","updated_at":"2026-07-28T15:40:19","tags":["concurrency","epoch-z","gate-integrity","gd-tests","pcb"],"value":"Unit Z1 (019fa83e8310) ran without a worktree per its brief. Mid-run, pcb/worker/tests/test_kicad_cli_boundary.py, test_rotation.py, tools/mutants/run_sweep.py were modified and a new tests/test_mutation_harness.py appeared (timestamps 08:34-08:36, during my pytest gate run) — not written by this unit. A concurrent sibling agent was editing pcb/worker/ in the SAME shared checkout at the same time. Consequence: the worker pytest gate result (1497 passed/13 failed/1 skipped) is NOT a clean-8ad726f measurement — it ran against a moving tree. Also separately observed: pcb/tests/gd/test_pcb_canvas_input_probe.gd reported \"0 total assertions\" in one full 30-suite run but passed cleanly standalone (33/33) immediately after — 1654+33=1687 matches the campaign's stated baseline total exactly, so this is a pre-existing flake (likely docket-DB/MCP-subprocess contention between sequential godot suite processes), not caused by any fix. Lesson: when a brief says \"no worktree\" for a unit whose gate touches shared mutable state (pcb/worker, pcb/tests/gd), treat any gate numbers gathered mid-wave as provisional and flag concurrent-edit evidence (mtimes) rather than trusting the raw pytest/gd summary.","component":"pcb-gd-tests","key":"shared-checkout-concurrency","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019fc504c69e74dfb37b65574967b419","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Go tests that mutate committed text fixtures break on Windows CI (CRLF)","created_at":"2026-08-03T00:27:32","updated_at":"2026-08-03T00:27:32","tags":["ci","crlf","fail-closed","testing","windows"],"value":"GitHub windows-latest checks out text files as CRLF (git's Windows core.autocrlf default). Go os.ReadFile is byte-exact, so strings.Replace(fixture, \"...\n\", ...) matches NOTHING there and silently returns the UNMUTATED fixture — the test then asserts against the wrong input and reads a correct success as a regression (real case: pcb TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_ExportAssembly, run 30773825956). Python is immune: pathlib.read_text() normalizes newlines, and dict-level mutations never touch text. FIX BOTH WAYS: (1) .gitattributes `*.yaml text eol=lf` in the plugin dir; (2) a helper that normalizes \\r\n->\n AND returns an error when the pattern matches nothing — a no-op mutation must fail closed, never pass through the original (see pcb/main_test.go replaceOnceLF). Repro on Linux: rewrite the fixture with CRLF on disk and run the test.","component":"go-tests","key":"crlf-fixture-mutation","confidence":"high","research_cost":12}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a007f1dd027c82ba9aebcb1e6da046","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"PCB assembly advisory sends the whole resolved board by value — exceeds the 64 KiB IPC cap on an ordinary 36-component board","description":"## What is actually wrong\n\nThe 64 KiB limit itself is CORRECT and is not the bug. `PluginScenePanelBroker.gd:157` sets `MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES := 65536`, and the plugin's Go side deliberately refuses at 60 KiB to stay clear of it (`internal/board/yaml.go:15-19`, citing gap register A-8). Both sides agree, the headroom is intentional, and it fails CLOSED (`status: \"indeterminate\"`, not a false pass) — which is the right failure mode and matches the plugin's own fail-safe policy.\n\n**The defect is that the payload exists at all.**\n\nThe board was loaded by `path` — it was read SERVER-SIDE and the bytes never needed to travel. The assembly advisory then serialises the whole RESOLVED board (116,260 bytes, roughly 6x the 20 KB source because pin geometry and resolved footprints are expanded) and pushes it back through a 64 KiB pipe. It is round-tripping data that is already on the other end.\n\n## Why this is severity 2 rather than a nitpick\n\nThe threshold is reachable by an ORDINARY board. 36 components on a 90x100mm two-layer board is small by any standard, and it is ~1.8x over the cap. That implies the assembly advisory is effectively unavailable for essentially any real board — it stops working precisely when boards become substantial enough to need checking.\n\n## Suggested fixes, cheapest first\n\n1. **Read the board server-side by handle** rather than passing it by value. This is the real fix — the panel already has the board.\n2. Return only the assembly FINDINGS rather than echoing the board back.\n3. Chunk the payload.\n\n(2) and (3) treat the symptom. Raising the 64 KiB cap is explicitly NOT proposed: it is a host transport constraint, it belongs to Minerva core rather than this plugin, and there is no evidence the cap is wrong — only that one consumer uses it wastefully.\n\n## Related\n\nDiscoverability of this failure in the GUI is filed separately — it is independently verifiable and independently fixable.","created_at":"2026-08-16T00:21:26","updated_at":"2026-08-19T22:04:09","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["assembly","ipc","load_board","payload","pcb","scaling"],"resolution":"The panel now projects boards to their canonical wire form (panel_tools.canonical_wire_board) at all three worker-channel seams, dropping render enrichment the worker re-derives from the library chain: 122,370 -> 13,259 bytes on smart-remote-v2, 20% of the 64 KiB broker cap. Prerequisite fix: the board_health completeness census is resolve-first, so canonical wire input yields an identical ledger (bug 01a01b6bc649). The assembly advisory and board_health now run on real boards for the first time. Uncommitted on the minerva-plugins dev tree; running via a dev-lane install.","environment":"Minerva PCB plugin panel (pcb_panel). Board: smart-remote-v2, 36 components / 29 nets, 90x100mm 2-layer, at ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/smart_remote_v2_board.yaml (511 lines, ~20 KB source). Observed 2026-08-15.","repro_steps":"1. minerva_pcb_load_board(editor_name=\"pcb_panel\", path=\"/home/imran/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/smart_remote_v2_board.yaml\")\n2. Read the reply's `assembly` field.\n\nACTUAL:\n  \"assembly\": {\"error\": \"Payload too large: 116260 bytes (limit: 65536 bytes)\",\n               \"status\": \"indeterminate\"}\n\nThe board loads fine (success:true, component_count:36, net_count:29) but the assembly advisory never runs."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a007f21ce37be183d0ff4757dfcc4f","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"PCB panel: a load-time check that returns indeterminate is invisible in the GUI — the human sees a clean load","description":"## The defect\n\nThe load reply is HONEST — it reports `status: \"indeterminate\"` with the reason, and the plugin's stated policy is that indeterminate is never a pass. The reporting is not the problem.\n\nThe problem is that this honesty exists ONLY on the MCP surface. On screen, a board whose assembly advisory silently failed to run is indistinguishable from one that passed.\n\nThis matters more than usual here because **the owner works from the GUI and does not call MCP tools**. A check that only announces its own unavailability through a JSON field is, for that user, a check that fails silently — which is the exact failure mode the fail-closed design exists to prevent. Fail-closed at the data layer plus invisible at the presentation layer nets out to fail-open for the human.\n\n## Why this is separate from the payload bug\n\nIndependent in both directions:\n\n- Fixing the payload size (bug 01a007f1dd027c82ba9aebcb1e6da046) makes THIS instance go away but does nothing for the NEXT check that cannot run for some other reason — worker unavailable, timeout, resolution failure.\n- Fixing the surfacing does not fix the payload.\n\nEach has its own acceptance test, so each can pass or fail on its own. Filing them as one item would produce something that can only ever come back half-passed.\n\n## Scope note\n\nThe fix should be general — driven by \"a load-time check reported indeterminate\", not special-cased to the assembly advisory — otherwise the same gap reopens for the next check.","created_at":"2026-08-16T00:21:42","updated_at":"2026-08-16T00:24:29","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["discoverability","fail-closed","gui-parity","load_board","pcb","ui"],"environment":"Minerva PCB plugin panel (pcb_panel). Reproduced with board smart-remote-v2 (36 components) on 2026-08-15, where the assembly advisory returned indeterminate due to an oversized payload.","repro_steps":"1. Load any board large enough that a load-time check cannot complete (smart-remote-v2 at ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/ reproduces it via the assembly advisory).\n2. Observe the MCP reply: it honestly carries {\"assembly\": {\"status\": \"indeterminate\", \"error\": \"...\"}}.\n3. Now look at the PANEL.\n\nACTUAL: the panel shows a normally-loaded board. Nothing indicates that a check did not run. A GUI-only user has no way to learn the advisory was skipped."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a00e8f4a6c7c34bbdec458cf105374","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"cad CI red since June: the runtime bundle was written to internal/runtime/bundle while go:embed reads internal/cadruntime/bundle","description":"FOUND 2026-08-16 while watching CI after the install-lane push. Pre-existing: every cad workflow run since at least 2026-06-23 failed at the same step for the same reason.\n\nCAUSE. 1c8291b (refactor(shared): extract plugin-agnostic bridge + runtime from cad, 2026-06-10) renamed cad/internal/runtime → cad/internal/cadruntime and moved the go:embed directives with it, but left BUNDLE_OUT_DIR in cad/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock pointing at the old path. That lock line was the LAST reference to internal/runtime anywhere in cad/ — grep confirms nothing else mentioned it. The destination directory's own .gitignore had been sitting unused in internal/cadruntime/bundle/ since the day of the rename.\n\nWHY IT HID FOR TWO MONTHS. The failure is not in the bundle step, which succeeds — it writes the tarball, lists the directory, and exits 0. The break surfaces one step later in `go build`, phrased as a go:embed pattern miss, which reads like a source problem rather than a path-config problem. codetools survived the same class of bug because its package is still internal/runtime, so its identical lock line is still correct.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. No cad release artifact could be produced for two months; the published cad-v0.1.2 assets were stale that whole time. Anyone installing cad from the marketplace got the pre-June binary.\n\nWORTH KEEPING: a build step that \"succeeds\" while writing to a path no consumer reads is invisible to exit codes. The binary-size assertion in cad.yml exists to catch an EMPTY embed; nothing asserted that the bundle landed where the embed looks. A cheap guard would be to have the bundle step verify its output directory matches the embed directory rather than just listing it.","created_at":"2026-08-17T07:11:06","updated_at":"2026-08-17T07:11:06","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["build","cad","ci","go-embed","packaging"],"resolution":"Fixed in d1f6c9d. cad/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock now sets BUNDLE_OUT_DIR=\"internal/cadruntime/bundle\"; cad.yml's two diagnostic `ls -la` lines and one stale comment updated to match. Verified end to end before pushing, not by inspection: the bundle script wrote a 237M tarball + sha256 into internal/cadruntime/bundle/, `go build ./` then succeeded, and the binary came out 240MB — above the 100MB floor CI's binary-size assertion uses to catch an empty go:embed. `go test ./...` green. CI run 31970922537 green on all three platforms, first green cad run since June, and the cad-v0.1.2 release assets refreshed for the first time since then.","repro_steps":"Before the fix: any push touching cad/ → GitHub Actions \"cad\" workflow → job \"cad (linux-x86_64)\" → step \"Build (native)\" fails with: internal/cadruntime/embed_linux_amd64.go:10:12: pattern bundle/runtime-bundle-linux-x86_64.sha256: no matching files found. Identical on macos-universal and windows-x86_64. The preceding step \"Build runtime bundle (single arch)\" reports SUCCESS — it wrote to a directory nothing embeds. Locally: go build -C cad -o /tmp/x ./ reproduces it on a tree whose bundle has not been built into the new path."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a01b4ae78472329a9e308a3d820690","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Panel to_board_dict is ~9x canonical size; worker channels re-resolve anyway","created_at":"2026-08-19T18:31:28","updated_at":"2026-08-19T22:04:28","tags":["assembly","ipc","payload","pcb"],"value":"pcb_component.gd to_board_dict() appends a render-detail tail (pads, graphics, local_bounds, properties, color) making the panel's board dict ~9x the canonical YAML size (smart-remote-v2: 137KB vs 15KB). The pcb.assembly_check and pcb.board_health channels DON'T need it: both inject withLibraryChain and land in methods._assembly_tri_state, which is resolve-first — it re-attaches courtyards/pads from the library itself. Sending the enriched dict is what blows the 64 KiB broker cap (bug 01a007f1dd02). Wire projections to worker channels should send canonical core + pins only (+pads only for non-library custom parts).","component":"pcb","key":"panel-board-dict-enrichment","research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a01b6bc6497b1e938712d2c1263e5a","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"board_health completeness census is not resolve-first — canonical-source callers get a false `complete: true`","description":"FOUND 2026-08-19 by the corpus-equivalence step of the 01a007f1dd02 verification ladder — the projection experiment tripped over it, but it is reachable TODAY with no projection involved.\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY. The board_health ledger has two halves with different input contracts:\n  * assembly half: methods._assembly_tri_state is RESOLVE-FIRST by documented contract — it calls _resolve_mapped(board, tolerant=True) itself, so a raw canonical board is enriched before measurement.\n  * completeness half: _completeness_keys reads the given dict VERBATIM. Pin copper is located from resolve-attached pad geometry; a canonical (unresolved) component with no inline pins contributes no pins to the census at all.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. _board_health_method's own docstring says \"params: {board: <canonical board dict>} (anything _load accepts)\" — but feed it actual canonical source and the census under-reports in the FAIL-OPEN direction. Measured on tests/testdata/gerber_boards/quadlayer.yaml (components carry ONLY library footprint refs, no inline pins):\n\n  board_health_method({yaml: quadlayer}) TODAY -> {\"complete\": true}\n  same board, enriched first                   -> {\"complete\": false,\n                                                   \"partial\": [{\"net\":\"SIG\",\"pin_groups\":2}],\n                                                   \"indeterminate\": [{\"net\":\"GND\",\"reason\":\"zone_copper\"}]}\n\n\"complete: true\" on a board with a split SIG net is a false pass — the exact vocabulary the tri-state census exists to make impossible. The Go channel description advertises canonical input verbatim, so any MCP-era caller sending {board:<canonical>} or {yaml:...} hits this.\n\nWHY IT HAS HIDDEN. The one production caller (PCBPanel load path) happens to send the deserialize-ENRICHED dict, so the census has always seen pads in practice. The contract and the caller disagree; the caller's accident masks the method's gap.\n\nFIX (2 lines, mirrors resolve_best_effort:461): _board_health_method should _resolve_mapped(board, tolerant=True, layers=_layer_params(params)) and run _board_health over the resolution, falling back to the raw board only when resolution errors — exactly what _assembly_tri_state already does internally. This is also the enabling prerequisite for the 01a007f1dd02 payload fix (lean canonical wire): once the census resolves-first, the panel may send canonical-core boards to BOTH channels.\n\nVERIFIED: with resolve-first simulated, board_health + assembly verdicts are dict-identical between enriched-dict input and lean canonical input across all 7 corpus boards (smart-remote-v2, gerber spike, coworking-unrouted/routed, assembly_fixture, drilltest, quadlayer).","created_at":"2026-08-19T19:07:23","updated_at":"2026-08-19T22:04:17","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["board-health","completeness","fail-open","parity","pcb","worker"],"resolution":"_board_health_method now resolves the board tolerantly (library chain) before the completeness census, exactly as the assembly half always did — canonical and enriched input yield the identical ledger, proven across the 7-board repo corpus plus a render-tail poison test. Fix + 13-check regression suite in worker/tests/test_board_health_resolve_first.py.","environment":"pcb worker, minerva-plugins main (2026-08-19). Found while running the verification ladder for payload bug 01a007f1dd02.","repro_steps":"cd ~/github/minerva-plugins/pcb/worker && .venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport sys; sys.path.insert(0,'.')\nfrom pcb_worker import methods\nr = methods._board_health_method({'yaml': open('tests/testdata/gerber_boards/quadlayer.yaml').read()})\nprint(r['result'].get('complete'))  # True — should be False (SIG in 2 pin_groups, GND zone_copper indeterminate)\n\""}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a01bbfd2827b0c94bfb79b9672955b","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Dev-lane install: app_userdata dir goes stale; data_directory is what runs","created_at":"2026-08-19T20:39:11","updated_at":"2026-08-19T22:04:30","tags":["dev-lane","gotcha","install","pcb","plugin"],"value":"After minerva_plugin_install on a DEV TREE manifest, app_userdata/plugins/<id>/ can keep the OLD files (stale binary + ui/) — do NOT read it to check whether a fix is live. The registry entry's data_directory points at the dev tree and that is what the host runs. Verify with /proc: `for p in $(pgrep -f <plugin>-plugin); do readlink -f /proc/$p/exe; done`. For pcb the dev binary (~11MB) has zero-byte runtime-bundle embeds so it falls through to worker/.venv (editable install) = live Python source; the release binary (~76MB) embeds its own frozen worker and can never pick up a source fix. ALSO: minerva_plugin_install may TIME OUT on the MCP call yet succeed — the dev lane runs the manifest setup stanza; check minerva_plugin_list instead of retrying.","component":"plugins","key":"dev-lane-install-truth","research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0223ec9e271269fd664fcf90dd20b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb.route (and serialize/deserialize) payloads exceed the 64KiB broker cap — move board out of the capped pipe via board_path + sha256 digest","description":"Observed on smart-remote-v2 (37 components, U1S = 44 THT pins, 29 nets): minerva_pcb_workspace_propose and minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints both fail with \"Payload too large: 153578 bytes (limit: 65536 bytes)\". Routing ONE 2-pin hint fails identically to routing everything — two attempts differed by 84 bytes — because PCBPanel.route_board (PCBPanel.gd:5370-5421) inlines _board_for_route_request() = _data.to_board_dict(), i.e. the ENTIRE draft-composed board, as obstacle context. The request dies at PluginScenePanelBroker.handle_scene_request step 6 (PluginScenePanelBroker.gd:609-620, MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES := 65536 at line 157; PluginWebviewBroker.gd:42 has the same cap).\n\nTHREE VICTIMS, not one: the same cap kills pcb.serialize export (documented at PCBPanel.gd:4804-4807) and large inline pcb.deserialize loads. Cap is one-directional (panel→broker requests only; replies and push_to_panel are uncapped) and platform-reserved channels (host.fs.watch, host_owned_save.response) already bypass it by early return.\n\nCHOSEN FIX (#2 of 6 graded candidates, weighted 94/105 on owner rubric durability→DRY→reliability→well-factored→readability→cost; table in session record 2026-08-20): board-by-path + digest. Panel snapshots the draft-composed board dict to a session temp file, sends {board_path, board_digest(sha256), route_hints, selection} — O(hints) not O(board). Python worker's board_model.load_board (board_model.py:25-49) grows a third arm: params[\"board_path\"] → read file, verify sha256 against board_digest (refuse loudly on mismatch), parse. load_board is the single resolver for route/drc/serialize consumers so the arm fixes all three victims; Go HandleRouteChannel forwards verbatim — zero Go changes; broker untouched — zero cross-plugin impact. Draft-composed board is panel-memory-only, so it must be SNAPSHOTTED, not referenced from the original YAML.\n\nRejected runners-up: broker blob-handle channel (76), manifest per-channel budget (60), worker session cache + deltas (56, cache-coherence trap), raise the cap (52, moves the cliff and masks the regression test), chunk/compress (52, wrong layer). Explicitly NOT doing raise-the-cap as interim. Spatially windowing the board is a correctness hazard — router fails closed on a board it cannot fully compile.\n\nRisks to cover in tests: temp-file lifecycle/cleanup, digest mismatch refusal, path encoding between Godot and Python worker, all three victim channels.","created_at":"2026-08-21T02:55:35","updated_at":"2026-08-21T03:49:47","priority":2,"tags":["broker","payload-limit","pcb","regression","router"],"resolution":"Board-by-reference shipped for route (plain + draft), serialize (both directions), deserialize, and the promote gate; snapshot + consumption helpers in panel_tools; worker + Go verified-digest arms. Follow-ups filed: sibling channels (01a0227029da), corpus-policy dct prose (01a0225bb250)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0225bb25078d19b181d7fa4c11fb4","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"corpus-policy: committed plugins.dct comment quotes the banned private-board path construction verbatim","description":"Pre-existing at HEAD (predates work item 01a0223ec9e271269fd664fcf90dd20b; verified by grepping `git show HEAD:docs/plugins.dct` — exactly one match, in committed comment id 27 on item 01a007f1dd027c82ba9aebcb1e6da046). The comment says the policy \"greps for exactly the <private-checkout>/smart-remote path construction\" — quoting the construction is itself a match. Comment rows are history; fix options: (a) a docket-native comment redaction/edit verb, (b) teach _PATH_REFERENCE to ignore .dct prose the way MINERVA_ROOT's docket is exempted (the scope comment in test_corpus_policy.py already reasons this way for Docs/minerva.dct), or (c) accept .dct in the repo-level exemption list. Option (b) matches the test's own stated rationale.","created_at":"2026-08-21T03:27:09","updated_at":"2026-08-21T04:09:29","severity":3,"tags":["corpus-policy","docket","pre-existing"],"resolution":".dct exemption added to the path-literal corpus-policy test; suite 13/13."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0227029da7b8b988ab93528925356","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Sibling board-carrying channels still ride the capped pipe inline: mask_view, fab_preview, board_health, assembly_check, draft_check","description":"Census from the fix cold review (finding 4). board_health/assembly_check are partially mitigated by the canonical wire-form projection (01a007f1dd02), but mask_view/fab_preview send the full board on exactly the class of board that blew pcb.route — WYSIWYG previews on smart-remote-v2-class boards will still die payload_too_large. Each is a one-line wiring through PCBPanel._payload_by_ref (their worker methods already resolve via the _load wrapper → board_model.load_board, which has the board_path arm); draft_check needs a look at whether {board, candidates} together clear the cap after the board goes by-ref. Add each wired channel to pcb/tests/gd/test_board_by_path.gd Section 1 and re-pin EXPECTED_SUITES.","created_at":"2026-08-21T03:49:31","updated_at":"2026-08-21T03:49:31","priority":3,"tags":["broker","follow-up","payload-limit","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a022ab356c77e2a838e81641533334","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"\"Proposal\" means ghost trace only: direct span-propose without a minted intent, task-scoped router bridge, vocabulary pass","description":"MOTIVATING SESSION EVIDENCE (smart-remote-v2 VBAT first leg, 2026-08-21): (1) owner asked \"don't make a proposal — ghost trace only\"; the only path was deleting the intent annotation after propose, which orphaned the surviving candidate — workspace_reroute_route then refused source_hints_missing because the panel router bridge can only scope a run by hint selection. (2) A placement nudge to straighten the route forced a full reject/delete/re-intent/re-propose cycle instead of a reroute. (3) Rejected-task residue: tasks whose hints are deleted linger open and unactionable.\n\nDESIGN SKETCH (three legs):\n(a) TASK-SCOPED ROUTER BRIDGE: scope router runs by task terminal sets (pin refs), not hint ids. The eager RouteTask already carries net + terminals and is the natural scoping object; hint ids become one way to select tasks, not the bridge's input contract. This also fixes orphaned-candidate reroutes and lets stale hint-less tasks be actionable or reapable.\n(b) DIRECT SPAN-PROPOSE: workspace_propose (or sibling arg/verb) accepts spans:[{source_pin, dest_pin, width_mm?}] — mints TASKS only, no annotation, lands ghost candidates. Intents (pcb_route_hint) remain the durable/citeable channel for corridor steering, width authority, and cross-session persistence — they become optional, not a toll booth.\n(c) VOCABULARY PASS: \"proposal\" = ghost candidate everywhere (status bar \"N proposals\", docs, tool descriptions); the dashed-pink annotation is labeled/rendered \"intent\" and never called a proposal. Canvas legend distinguishes intent (ask), ghost (proposed answer), copper (committed).\n\nCONTEXT: C4b already retired proposal ANNOTATIONS (propose lands candidates, writes nothing) — the object model is 80% there; the gaps are the mandatory intent mint, the hint-only bridge scoping, and mixed labels. Cold-review finding from work item 01a0223ec9e2 noted the bridge limitation independently.\n\nSTATUS: proposed; awaiting option scoring (owner rubric: durability → DRY → reliability → well-factored → readability → cost) with independent fable review before designing.","created_at":"2026-08-21T04:54:00","updated_at":"2026-08-21T05:53:30","tags":["pcb","proposal-vocabulary","routing","ux","workspace"],"resolution":"B+C+D shipped: spans one-call span-propose (union, atomic, minted_hint_ids), hint-less reroute fallback (same-task supersession, endpoints carried, honest degrade + steering refusal), vocabulary (Intents header, docs, MCP manifest). Open: owner veto window on the UNION ruling; worker-side warning wording follow-up (comment 31)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a022b1b7d571d594e8f6ddfb3c8bce","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Routing sidecar zero-payload rule loses task-owned corridor constraints for unanswered intents across save/reload","description":"Found by the independent design review of DCR 01a022ab356c (P5). Live data loss today, independent of the DCR's fate: author intent with corridor → save → reload → corridor gone, next propose runs unguided with no warning. Fix direction: the zero-payload rule must count durable tasks (or at least tasks carrying a routing_constraint) as payload. Blast radius: test_workspace_persistence.gd pins the sidecar round-trip — grow it with an unanswered-constrained-intent case proven red first. This bug also gates any future task-native ontology work (the deferred legs of DCR 01a022ab356c): tasks minted without annotations cannot survive a session until this is fixed.","created_at":"2026-08-21T05:01:07","updated_at":"2026-08-21T05:01:07","severity":2,"tags":["data-loss","pcb","persistence","routing-workspace","sidecar"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a026ddd3ee72a9b45ec33ec9cf23d9","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"EPOCH SR2FAB — clear the plugin-side defects between smart-remote-v2 and a gated promote","description":"PURPOSE: clear every defect standing between the live smart-remote-v2 board and a gated promote -> gerbers/BOM/CPL, and only what is cheap to carry alongside that chain.\n\nAuthored by Fable from source (v0.4.2, main @ f74db1b). Opus implements.\n\nREVISION NOTE: supersedes the first draft (14 stations, \"SR2-FAB\"). Rewritten after two owner corrections — Godot syntax checking is exempt from testex, and the board's data is safe.\n\n== ENVIRONMENT FACTS (owner, 2026-08-21) ==\n- LINUX DESKTOP, not the Mac laptop.\n- The drive plugin syncs Minerva projects to the drive service. BOARD DATA CANNOT BE DESTROYED. Fable's original framing of S1 as an urgent \"board rescue\" is WRONG — S1 stays first because it closes a real capability gap and needs no godot invocation, not because the board is at risk.\n- Godot's editor is closed; Minerva is shut down.\n- Canonical YAML on disk is unchanged since 80c829c (traces: [], keepouts only). Tonight's copper — Q1 rotation+freeform position, 11 traces, 4 vias, the GND pour — lives in the Minerva project (drive-synced). Two untracked sidecars exist: .annotations.json (6.7KB) and .routing.json (58KB, workspace candidates/tasks, board_revision 51). The sidecars hold the WORKSPACE, not committed copper.\n\n== EXECUTION MODEL (fixed) ==\nOpus implements S0-S12 -> Fable single end-of-epoch review -> <=3 fix rounds -> Codex review via docket question (this project) -> <=3 fix rounds -> testex (FIRST execution of any test, pytest included).\n\n**SERIAL EXECUTION ONLY (owner ruling).** No station runs in parallel with another. No parallel subagents implementing. One station at a time, in order.\n\nRULES:\n- Tests AUTHORED but NOT EXECUTED until testex. Owner ruling; inverts the standing \"tests must RUN when written\" rule deliberately.\n- Godot SYNTAX checking exempt and continuous (S0 governs how).\n- Python has NO execution exemption. OPEN QUESTION FOR OWNER: extend the allowance to `python -m py_compile` only (pure parser, no imports, no pytest) — the exact analogue of Godot --check-only. Would convert S2/S3/S11's \"syntax unchecked\" rows to ~0.\n- COMMENTS: new comments minimal, functional only. No status, owner-request, or docket/DCR references in NEW comments. Forward-only — existing decision-trail comments untouched, including hunks that merely brush them. No stripping sweep.\n- DELIVERY: merge to main only. No version bump, no marketplace cut. (Whenever a release happens: main.go serverVersion and manifest.json version move in lockstep — that mismatch forced the 0.4.2 re-cut at a007ffb.)\n- OPERATIONAL: Python worker changes deploy via backend restart (the worker is the Go plugin's subprocess; _start_and_verify_backend, ui/PCBPanel.gd:4803-4808). The GD panel and in-memory board are untouched by a worker restart. Panel GDScript hot-reloads from the dev-lane data_directory.\n\n== STATIONS (serial) ==\n\nS0 — Pinned-safe syntax-check invocation. Prerequisite. S.\nEstablish ONE verified-safe per-file GDScript parse check (candidate: godot --headless --check-only --script <file> against a DISPOSABLE scratch project, never --path at any Minerva project). Record the incantation as the epoch's standing rule with explicit prohibitions: no --path <minerva project>, no --import, gd-runner only with --preflight-only (a FLAG, not an env var). No station runs any godot invocation before S0 closes.\nTests: none (operational prerequisite).\n\nS1 — minerva_pcb_export_yaml. Closes M1's practical harm, F3. S. Deps: none.\nGD-only verb reusing the export button's plumbing verbatim — _on_export_yaml_pressed (ui/PCBPanel.gd:4823-4860), _payload_by_ref (ui/PCBPanel.gd:4817) — as minerva_pcb_export_yaml {path?}: dispatch entry (ui/panel_tools.gd:100-292), manifest.json tools entry, reply labelled draft:true. Bump PLUGIN_BUILD (ui/PCBPanel.gd:77) in the same edit — stale since it landed, by its own design-note admission (ui/PCBPanel.gd:57-76).\nWHY FIRST (corrected): not a rescue — the drive plugin protects the data. First because it closes the only gap that makes a gate-blocked board unwritable to canonical YAML, needs no godot invocation (panel hot-reloads), and gets the board's copper into ccsandbox git where the rest of the epoch can reference it.\nTests: gd (headless, fake IPC) for dispatch + refusal shapes, mirroring tests/gd/test_board_by_path.gd's by-ref assertions.\nRisk: syntax ~0; behaviour unverified until testex — reviewer reads the by-ref wrap and _unwrap_channel_reply layering (ui/panel_tools.gd:5259-5279) for envelope parity with the button path.\n\nS2 — E1: model zone_connect. Closes E1, missed-9. THE BLOCKER. M. Deps: none.\nCapture the (zone_connect n) VALUE in the parser (footprints.py:342, footprint_def.py:_raw_marker at :324-326). Rewrite DefaultCapabilityPolicy.is_blocking (compile_board.py:278-291): values 0/1/2 non-blocking with per-value warnings — v1 fill is SOLID-connect only (zone_fill.py:35-36, 838-845), so 0/2 are modeled exactly and 1 is electrically-connected-but-solid, advisory. Value 3 or unparsable blocks ONLY WHEN RELEVANT: replace board-wide _declares_copper_pour (compile_board.py:301-320) with pad-scoped _pour_could_touch — non-keepout zone, same net, on a copper layer the pad occupies.\nOn this board U2's EP is TOP and the pour is BOTTOM: under the gate the marker is not even relevant. That is the epoch's unblocking event.\nTests: 4-quadrant pytest matrix (0/1/2/3 x relevant/irrelevant pour) + a fixture encoding this board's shape (thermal-pad QFN top, GND pour bottom -> compiles; value-3 same-net same-layer -> blocks). Homes: worker/tests/test_compile_board.py, test_footprint_def.py.\nRISK: HIGHEST-DANGER STATION. Python, syntax AND behaviour inspection-only. An error here silently fail-OPENS the one genuinely corrupting case (value 3). MANDATED SUBSTITUTE: a written truth table (value x pour-relevance -> verdict) mapped line-by-line in the review request, plus a post-testex operational replay (compile + board_check) before any promote is attempted.\n\nS3 — D3: pad layers in the connectivity projection. Closes D3. S/M. Deps: none in code; reviewed alongside S2.\nconnectivity_board emits SMD pads' copper layers (ir_connectivity.py:92-101 currently emits bare pins); carry `layers` through the pad-source fallback (pad_source.py:748-752, 789). drc._Pad.occupies already consumes them (drc.py:208-218); permissive-when-undeclared stays for TH pads by physical correctness.\nVOCABULARY TRAP: _harvest_pads folds KiCad spellings (\"F.Cu\", via kicad_to_canon at drc.py:299-300), so the projection must emit KiCad names, NOT the canonical \"top\" the rest of ir_connectivity speaks (ir_connectivity.py:123).\nTests: pytest in test_drc.py — F.Cu-only SMD pad + bottom segment at its center -> 2 pin groups; + through via -> 1; projection emits layers for SMD, none for TH.\nRISK: HIGH. Flips census verdicts BOTH ways; a false-partial would block legitimate promotes. Review must reason about the TH-permissive branch and the *.Cu spanning case (drc.py:295-303). Expect NEW TRUE FINDINGS on this board post-fix; that counts as success.\n\nS4 — Optional-tier DRC floors become real. Closes missed-2. S. Deps: none.\nJSON-only edit to library/profiles/v1-fab-conservative.json (currently ten floor keys): add min_silk_width_mm, min_silk_to_pad_mm, min_hole_to_copper_mm WITH PUBLISHED JLC FIGURES CITED IN THE PROFILE'S `source` STRING.\nCORRECTION vs the first draft: min_hole_to_edge_mm STAYS ABSENT. No house publishes one, and the K21 doctrine (design minima only from a sanctioned, cited origin — compile_board.py:233-247) forbids invented numbers. drc_geometric.py:2032-2039 documents this deliberately.\nTurns gc9/gc10 zeros from \"the profile said nothing\" into real verdicts (drc_geometric.py:1110-1114).\nTests: pytest asserting the loaded profile carries the three floors and that gc9/gc10 produce findings on a crafted violating fixture.\nRisk: LOW. JSON provable by reading; the load path (manufacturer_profile.load_rule_profile, eager at import — compile_board.py:250-259) is Python and unexecuted, so a typo'd key fails module import at testex, loudly.\n\nS5 — B2 minimal: never overwrite a declared segment width. Closes B2. B1 deferred. S. Deps: none.\nmethods.py:2323 seg[\"width_mm\"] = width -> seg.setdefault(\"width_mm\", width). The overlay's precedence (ir_candidates.py:368-370) already prefers the segment's value. Add subject_widths_mm to gc2 finding rows (drc_geometric.py:1736-1790) so any future phantom is self-diagnosing.\nTests: pytest — a candidate segment declaring 0.5 under a 1.0 run width yields the same gc2 verdict as a standalone 0.5 check. Kills the phantom-violation AND the symmetric FALSE-CLEAN direction.\nRisk: LOW — one line, arguable from the ir_candidates precedence chain on the page.\n\nS6 — A4: draft-check un-broken. Closes A4, heals B3. S/M. Deps: S1.\nGD half: check_draft wraps its payload by-ref before emit (PCBPanel.gd:5711 inline board, :5741 emit; copy the route_board precedent at :5465) and returns the broker failure dict instead of collapsing to {}, so _workspace_check (panel_tools.gd:7986, error at :8029) can name draft_check_refused / draft_check_timeout / draft_overlay_drifted instead of one conflated draft_check_no_reply.\nPython half: _draft_check (methods.py:3087-3088) gains the ten-line board_path+digest inflation copied from _promote_check (methods.py:859-869, via board_model.load_board, board_model.py:45-71), fail-closed on BoardParseError.\nWHY IT SURVIVES THE CUT: mhroute owns final routing, but the human+agent finishing pass (pour edge, keepouts, GND stitching) happens in-editor, and committing unverifiable ghosts is how boards get wrecked.\nTests: gd by-ref assertion for pcb.draft_check (the exact hole in test_board_by_path.gd's coverage); pytest draft_check with board_path; gd test for the de-conflated error names against tests/gd/test_workspace_check.gd's fake-panel scaffold.\nRisk: GD syntax ~0; behaviour (live broker hop) unverifiable until testex. Reviewer traces the envelope through all three unwrap layers and checks by-ref key symmetry (board -> board_path/board_digest) against both consumers. Python half LOW (verbatim pattern copy).\n\nS7 — D2: degenerate copper guards. Closes D2. S. Deps: S1.\nGD: _commit_preflight (pcb_routing_workspace.gd:2129-2151) drops consecutive duplicate points within COPPER_COINCIDENT_EPS_MM and refuses segments collapsing under 2 distinct points (ERR_UNMODELABLE_SEGMENT, named); same filter at candidate ingest (pattern exists: _bus_drop_duplicates, panel_tools.gd:8168-8180).\nPython: compile_board.py:1866 degenerate-trace message gains the trace ID beside the ordinal, so a stranded board is repairable via delete_traces.\nTests: gd preflight test with a start==end segment — a mutation that survives today's entire suite; pytest for the id-bearing message.\nRisk: GD syntax ~0, behaviour low (pure model-path validation, no broker); Python trivial.\n\nS8 — M4: mounting-hole read verb + placement advisory. Closes M4. S. Deps: S10 for the snap helper (or land the helper here if S10 trails).\nGD: minerva_pcb_list_holes reading pcb_data.gd:108 mounting_holes [{position, diameter, plated}] (serialize refs :2608/:2918), snapped; dispatch + manifest.json entry; reply includes a placement advisory naming coincident or >=3-collinear holes — the exact shape of the prior silent-rewrite incident, which NO gc check catches (gc11 proximity never runs, drc_geometric.py:2032-2039; gc10 undeclared pre-S4; gc6 only on near-collision).\nTests: gd — verb reply shape; advisory fires on a 4-holes-same-y fixture, silent on the rectangle pattern.\nRisk: GD syntax ~0; behaviour low (pure read).\n\nS9 — M3: declared-intent honesty surfacing. Closes M3's residual foot-gun. S. Deps: none.\nPython: in _promote_check's advisory block (methods.py:923-938) add a stage-incongruence advisory (\"stage defers routing but N traces exist\").\nGD: board_check/promote replies headline complete_by_declaration instead of a bare complete:true; get_layout_state (panel_tools.gd:5101) gains fabrication_stage.\nNO GATE CHANGE — the granular-promotion and declared-intent rulings stand.\nTests: pytest for the advisory tri-state; gd for the layout-state key.\nRisk: Python LOW (additive advisory), GD ~0/low.\n\nS10 — F4: millimetre quantization at the API boundary. Closes F4. S. Deps: before S11.\nGD: one _mm(v) = snapped(v, 0.0001) helper at every mm-emitting reply (_get_pin_position panel_tools.gd:430-438 is the worst offender; get_components, describe_component, get_selection, candidate geometry, spatial_query echoes — _list_vias :3757-3758 is the existing correct pattern) AND at authored-input ingest (add_trace/add_via), so what mhroute reads and writes back land on one grid.\nWHY CORE: the manufacture route is external Manhattan routing consuming pin positions over MCP; float32 residue (75.4000015258789) is DRC noise and S11 false-positives waiting to happen.\nTests: gd — pad at x=75.4 serializes as 75.4; ingest snap round-trip.\nRisk: GD syntax ~0; behaviour low but WIDE — reviewer's job is completeness of the sweep; a missed emitter re-opens the class silently.\n\nS11 — D1: gc12 trace-direction rule. Closes D1. M. Deps: S10 (tolerance must sit above the snap grid), S4 (same profile-extension pattern).\nPython: new gc12_trace_direction in drc_geometric.py (registry :2281-2295, dispatch :2423-2434): per segment, angle to nearest allowed axis <= tolerance; findings carry measured_angle_deg + endpoint.\nCORRECTION vs the first draft: profile-gated and ABSENT-BY-DEFAULT via a NEW profile (v1-fab-manhattan) or board-level design_rules opt-in. The shared conservative profile must NOT silently impose Manhattan on every board (selection path: compile_board.py:240-247). smart-remote-v2's YAML opts in — a cross-repo operational step in ccsandbox, not a plugin change.\nTests: angle unit vectors — exact-H, exact-V, 45deg, the real 0.9deg-off-vertical case, and a conforming segment with sub-grid jitter.\nRisk: Python, inspection-only, MEDIUM — new check class; fail direction must be \"spurious finding\", never \"missed diagonal\" (the module's cardinal rule, drc_geometric.py:11-25).\n\nS12 — F1: static-warning aggregation. Closes F1. S. Deps: none; last because everything else changes what the warnings ARE.\nPython: at the _promote_check/board_check reply boundary, group the repeated compile diagnostics (feature_omitted per marker, captured_geometry_not_emitted per component — attach points methods.py:213-220, 299-307) into {code, count, refs[:N]} rows + a static_warnings_digest; full flat list behind verbose_warnings:true. Sort before hash.\nWHY CORE: the DRC-clean push runs board_check dozens of times; this is the single largest context cost of coworking on the manufacture loop.\nTests: pytest — grouped shape, digest stability across identical calls, verbose passthrough.\nRisk: Python LOW-MED — the panel status line consumes these replies; reviewer checks both consumers by reading.\n\n== ORDERING RATIONALE ==\n1. S0 -> S1 first. S1 is GD-only and hot-reloads, needing no godot invocation.\n2. S2 (E1) is the keystone, immediately after: every compile-consuming capability is dead until it lands. It is also the most dangerous inspection-only change, so it gets maximum soak time before review.\n3. S3 with S2: together they make the promote gate's two halves honest; reviewing them as a pair keeps the fail-closed story coherent.\n4. Cheap provable-by-inspection work next (S4, S5) per the testex rule — near-zero review cost, and they de-risk everything measured later.\n5. S6-S9 are the GD-heavy middle, moved earlier than pre-addendum: syntax risk is now ~0, leaving only behaviour reasoning.\n6. S10 before S11 (non-obvious): an angle rule authored before reply/ingest quantization would need its tolerance chosen against float32 noise; snapping first lets the tolerance be a fab-meaningful number.\n7. S12 last: it summarizes warnings whose content S2/S4 change.\n\n== GATES ==\nLeave any station: code + authored tests in the diff; per-station inspection note written; GD files pass the S0-pinned parse check; NO execution of any authored test.\nLeave S2 specifically: the truth table (value x relevance -> verdict) written and mapped to lines; value-3-relevant provably still blocks by reading.\n\nEPOCH EXIT (after testex):\n1. All authored suites green (pytest + gd) within the <=3 + <=3 fix-round budget.\n2. Operational replay on the board: YAML on disk and adopted; compiles with the pour present; board_check geometric verdict DETERMINATE with zero unsupported_feature; gc9/gc10 running against cited floors; connectivity census honest per S3 (new true findings count as success).\n3. Fab-preview raster inspected via the existing live path (set_view show_fab_preview + get_image — ui/PCBPanel.gd:454, 4175-4185); gerbers/drills/BOM/CPL emit without identity refusals.\n4. EXPLICITLY NOT an exit criterion: the 23 unrouted nets. The epoch delivers a VERIFIABLE PATH TO PROMOTE; routing completion is the mhroute campaign's job, with GND required trace+via-complete for the census (the CPN1 narrowing, drc.py:797-805) until the pour-modeling epoch lands.\n\n== CUT LIST ==\nB1 full per-hint width provenance -> Workspace-ergonomics. mhroute owns final routing; S5 removes the correctness hazard.\nC1/C2/C3 reroute blast radius, constraint survival, task orphaning -> Workspace-ergonomics. Off this board's manufacture path.\nA1/A2/A3 proposed-via / selection geometry / spatial visibility -> Workspace-ergonomics. Coworking polish, not fab-gating.\nF2 live-editor drc_geometric/drc -> Workspace-ergonomics. board_check (+S12) covers the live board.\nE2 rotate direction swap -> Workspace-ergonomics. Needs a visual confirmation that mutates the live board; not fab-gating.\nM2 fill-aware pour connectivity -> Pour-modeling. Real engineering on the fill/census seam; the interim rule (GND trace-complete) suffices for promote.\nMissed-1 floating-pour detection -> Pour-modeling. Falls out of M2's fill-aware census.\nM9 BOM order_qty -> Assembly epoch if the workaround chafes. Zero-code workaround exists NOW: model U1S as two grouped components (_bom_rows qty = ref count, assembly_outputs.py:331; groups are \"one physical part\"). Operational modeling task on the board, not plugin code.\nM6 vector-level fab preview over MCP -> Assembly/preview epoch. Raster path suffices.\nMissed-3/-7/-8 (thermal-refusal UX, keepout asymmetry surfacing, fiducials) -> Pour-modeling / assembly. Advisory-grade; the fiducial decision is the owner's board-level call.\n\n== FABLE'S END-OF-EPOCH REVIEW CHECKLIST (step 2) ==\n1. Register closure map — each claimed item traced to its diff hunk; nothing closed by assertion.\n2. Seam symmetry — by-ref pairs complete on both sides (S1, S6: GD wrap <-> worker inflation, key names byte-equal); profile keys (S4, S11) byte-equal between JSON and their Python readers.\n3. Fail-closed audits — S2: quadrant table vs code, value-3-relevant blocks, unparsable blocks; S3: TH and *.Cu pads stay permissive; S11: fail direction is spurious-finding.\n4. Test-authorship audit BY READING (nothing ran): for every authored test, would it fail against pre-fix code? Any test passing both sides is returned as a defect. Fixture reachability reasoned per the mutate-to-find-fixture-gaps rule.\n5. COMMENT SALIENCE (named dimension): new comments minimal, functionality ONLY, absent where the code speaks; ZERO status/owner-request/docket/DCR references in new comments; FORWARD-ONLY COMPLIANCE BOTH DIRECTIONS — no stripping or rewording of existing docket/DCR/ruling comments anywhere in edited files, including hunks that merely brush them. Violations in either direction are review findings.\n6. Diff hygiene — no version bump, no manifest.json version change, no drive-by reformatting; dispatch/manifest/tool-schema entries consistent for every new verb.\n7. Behaviour-reasoning notes present — each GD station's \"what could only be verified by running\" note exists and is specific (envelope layer, dispatch route, hot-reload assumption).\n\n== CANNOT-VERIFY-UNTIL-TESTEX REGISTER ==\nS1, S6-gd, S7-gd, S8, S9-gd, S10 — syntax ~0 (S0-pinned checker); behaviour: broker envelope unwrapping, dispatch wiring, reply shapes, hot-reload assumptions; S10's sweep completeness.\nS2 — syntax UNCHECKED (Python); behaviour: the whole adjudication truth table. HIGHEST DANGER; inspection substitute mandated.\nS3 — unchecked; census verdict flips in BOTH directions.\nS5, S6-py, S9-py — unchecked; LOW (pattern copies and one-liners).\nS4 — JSON readable; profile import path fails loudly at testex import.\nS11 — unchecked; MEDIUM (new check class geometry math).\nS12 — unchecked; reply-shape change with two consumers.\n\n== CODEX REVIEW REQUEST (step 4) ==\nFile as a docket QUESTION against this project (plugins.dct — the repo's only .dct, established home of pcb DCRs), self-contained: registers summarized, station table, the S2 truth table inline, commit SHA pinned. Owner shuttles the verdict.","created_at":"2026-08-22T00:27:47","updated_at":"2026-08-22T07:52:29","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["drc","epoch","fabrication","pcb","smart-remote-v2"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a026ec0d807d1c81dea0011ae28b61","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Safe per-file GDScript syntax check that never opens a Minerva project","created_at":"2026-08-22T00:43:19","updated_at":"2026-08-22T00:43:19","tags":["gdscript","godot","pcb","syntax","testex","tooling"],"value":"pcb/scripts/gd-syntax-check.sh <file.gd>... | --all   (exit 0 clean, 1 parse error, 2 harness). ~0.35s/file; whole tree (78 files) 27s.\n\nIt runs `godot --headless --path <disposable scratch> --check-only --script res://../../minerva-plugins/<rel>`, so it NEVER touches a Minerva checkout — unlike scripts/run-gd-tests.sh, which uses `--path <minerva>/src` and kills a running Minerva.\n\nLoad-bearing fact (measured, Godot 4.6.2): parsing precedes resolution and ABORTS before it. A file with both a syntax error and an unresolvable base class prints ONLY the syntax error. So a file whose diagnostics are all resolver-family is proven free of parse errors — no host stubs needed, and stubs would invent false errors of their own.\n\nResolver-family noise in the scratch project (all seven observed across the tree): Could not find base class / Could not find type / Could not resolve script / Cannot infer the type of X (variable|constant) / Identifier X not declared / Failed to compile depended scripts / Preload file X does not exist. All are caused by Minerva class_name types (MinervaPluginPanel, AnnotationKind) and res://test/helpers preloads that only exist in Minerva's project.\n\nOne is re-promoted to a real defect: a missing preload whose path points back into minerva-plugins. That catches typo'd in-repo preload paths, the usual way new dispatch wiring fails.\n\nDoes NOT prove the file resolves, loads or behaves. That needs the real host.\n\nPython analogue: python3 -m py_compile <files> — worker tree compiles in 4.5s; __pycache__ is gitignored.","component":"pcb","key":"tooling.gd-syntax-check","confidence":"high","research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0274b0e9a7f188057e28306d73027","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"CODEX REVIEW — EPOCH SR2FAB @ 0aa3d96: 13 stations, nothing executed","description":"REPO github.com/imrans-lab/minerva-plugins · BRANCH epoch/sr2fab · PINNED SHA 0aa3d96f4bf776d6c269e995d02c36bf48678c5c · DIFF `git diff main...0aa3d96` (17 commits).\n\nTHE ONE THING TO KNOW BEFORE READING ANYTHING ELSE: **nothing in this branch has been executed.** By owner ruling the epoch authored tests and never ran them. The only gates that ran are `python -m py_compile`, a per-file `godot --check-only` (pcb/scripts/gd-syntax-check.sh, added by this branch), and `go build ./...`. No pytest, no gd suite, no live board. So every behavioural claim below is reasoned from reading, and the review that matters most is the one that asks \"would this actually do that\".\n\nThat is not hypothetical. Fable's review found `math.isfinite` used in compile_board.py with no `import math` — a NameError that py_compile passes happily and that would have made the whole of station S11 inert. Assume there are more of that class.\n\n== WHAT THE EPOCH IS FOR ==\nsmart-remote-v2 is a real board being pushed to fabrication. Every compile-consuming capability on it was dead: one `(zone_connect 2)` marker on a VQFN exposed pad refused the entire board whenever it declared a copper pour, taking geometric DRC, the routing IR and promotion with it. The epoch clears that and the defects standing beside it.\n\n== STATIONS ==\nS0  build   safe per-file GDScript syntax check that never opens a Minerva project\nS1  feat    minerva_pcb_export_yaml — live board as YAML text, ungated, writes nothing\nS2  fix     zone_connect judged by VALUE then by REACH  <- the unblocking event\nS3  fix     connectivity projection emits pad copper layers\nS4  fix     a zero count now says whether the rule was MEASURED (planned body CANCELLED)\nS5  fix     a segment keeps a width it declared for itself\nS6  fix     draft check goes by-ref and names its own faults\nS7  fix     a segment whose two ends are one point is not copper\nS8  feat    minerva_pcb_list_mounting_holes + placement advisory\nS9  fix     say which kind of \"complete\" a board reached\nS10 fix     millimetre values leave the surface on one grid\nS11 feat    GC12 trace direction, board-gated, absent by default\nS12 perf    collapse repeated compile warnings, with a stable digest\n+3 fix rounds against Fable's review (f9771e3, d0e336c, 0aa3d96).\n\n== S2's ADJUDICATION TABLE, THE HIGHEST-DANGER CHANGE ==\nRelevance := some non-keepout zone, on the pad's net, on a copper layer the pad occupies.\n\n  value        | relevance              | pad kind | verdict | why\n  -------------+------------------------+----------+---------+------------------------------\n  2            | any                    | any      | ALLOW   | v1's native solid connect\n  3            | relevant               | surface  | ALLOW   | 3 == 2 on an SMD/connect pad\n  3            | relevant               | thru     | BLOCK   | 3 == 1 on a through-hole pad\n  1            | relevant               | any      | BLOCK   | spokes; solid fill = cold joint\n  0            | relevant               | any      | BLOCK   | isolation inverted by solid fill\n  unreadable   | relevant               | any      | BLOCK   | uninterpretable instruction\n  0/1/3/unread | irrelevant             | any      | ALLOW   | nothing for the style to act on\n  any non-2    | context unreadable     | any      | BLOCK   | <-- MUST NEVER FAIL OPEN\n  any non-2    | footprint-level marker | pad=None | board-wide relevance\n\nCode: compile_board.py `_zone_connect_blocks` / `_pour_could_touch` / `_pad_occupies` / `_pad_net_name`. THE PLAN SAID 0 AND 1 SHOULD WARN; I made them block, because v1's fill connects same-net copper SOLID and nothing else, so 0 (isolate) and 1 (thermal spokes) are both silently overridden — and zone_fill._refuse_thermal already REFUSES the identical intent at zone scope. Warning at pad scope while refusing at zone scope would make strictness depend on where the author typed it. Tell me if that is wrong; it is the epoch's most consequential judgement call.\n\nNet effect is a LOOSENING: pre-branch, any zone_connect value blocked board-wide whenever any pour existed.\n\n== FIVE PLACES I DEPARTED FROM THE PLAN ==\n1. S4's body CANCELLED, not deferred. It called for writing JLCPCB's published silk/hole-to-copper figures into v1-fab-conservative.json — a profile whose own `source` says it is \"not a specific board house\". That falsifies its provenance or asserts JLC is a cross-house floor, and there is no second source to triangulate (OSH Park publishes neither figure). It would also have retroactively re-verdicted pcb/fab/coupon-001, already ordered and DFM-passed. Shipped instead: `not_evaluated`, which names every rule whose floor the profile does not declare, so a count of 0 says whether it was measured. Closes the class for every profile including permanently-silent ones.\n2. S11 gated on BOARD state (design_rules.allowed_trace_angles_deg), not on a rule profile, for the same reason: no board house requires orthogonal routing.\n3. S10's ingest-side snap NOT implemented. Measured: `snapped(v, 0.0001)` stored back into a Vector2 returns the float32 unchanged, so an ingest snap is dead code shaped like a guarantee — and snapping an author's off-grid input would silently move their copper. The grid only holds at the reply boundary.\n4. S8's verb is `list_mounting_holes`, not `list_holes` — pad drills and via barrels are holes too.\n5. PLUGIN_BUILD bumped once at S10, not per station.\n\n== WHERE I MOST EXPECT TO BE WRONG ==\n* **The NameError class.** F1 proves our gates cannot see it. Sweep for names used but not imported/defined, Python and GDScript, across the whole diff.\n* **S10's sweep completeness.** Three rounds, three different enumeration methods, and each found emitters the previous missed (candidate geometry as bare `[x,y]` arrays; then `_pin_info`'s position, one screen from the one already fixed; then `_move_relative`'s snap-disclosure `requested`). Assume a fourth exists.\n* **Tests that pass for the wrong reason.** Three were caught this way — one deleted (a via credit that the cross-layer coincident-endpoint rule was actually providing), one fixture that could not see a reorder bug it was written to catch, one whose duplicate pair sat in the position the pre-fix code already skipped. Fable's checklist rule 4 says any test passing both sides is a defect; audit for more.\n* **Hand-derived assertion pins.** test_draft_composer.gd 91->95 and test_pcb_panel_layout.gd 149->150, both derived from counting added assertions, never from a run.\n\n== THREE THINGS READING COULD NOT SETTLE (Fable's list, and I agree) ==\n1. Run the mutation proof rather than trusting the walkthrough: execute tests/gd/test_mounting_holes_verb.gd at this SHA AND with the any-pair guard reverted, and confirm cell (f) actually goes red pre-fix.\n2. Wire-level residue: read live MCP replies (pin_info, move_component, list_mounting_holes) and confirm the snapped values serialize clean through GDScript's JSON float printing and the Go/broker path — that nothing downstream reintroduces residue or re-truncates through a float32.\n3. Consumer audit ACROSS REPOS: the quantize-replies / verbatim-model boundary is only safe if no consumer computes copper against the verbatim surfaces. Does mhroute (ccsandbox tools/mhroute/) or any agent workflow read journal coordinates, `requested`, or feed reply positions back as authored geometry? That spans repos neither of us read.\n\n== CONTEXT ==\nPlan of record and all owner rulings: this project, item 01a026ddd3ee72a9b45ec33ec9cf23d9 (description = the plan; comment 32 = owner rulings; comments 33-44 = per-station notes stating every deviation and its argument).\n\nCI NOTE worth knowing: a description mismatch between pcb/manifest.json and internal/tools/worker_tools.go fails the Go test step BEFORE the Python suite runs, so the job goes red while pytest never executes. This branch touched that pair; all 19 backend-executor tools were verified byte-identical on both description and input_schema.\n\n== NOT IN SCOPE ==\nThe board's 23 unrouted nets. The epoch delivers a verifiable path to promote; routing completion belongs to the mhroute campaign.","created_at":"2026-08-22T02:27:05","updated_at":"2026-08-22T07:13:29","directed_to":"codex","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["codex","epoch","pcb","review","smart-remote-v2"],"resolution":"APPROVED at 085e8cf. Three rounds: 4 findings, then 2 residuals, then approve. No blocking code-review findings remain. Approval covers CODE REVIEW ONLY and explicitly does not waive the operational exit gate — compile, board_check, fab preview and gerbers/BOM/CPL still need replay against the live smart-remote-v2 board. One non-blocking hygiene item (_arr_pair's dead quantization) filed as chore 01a028512e26 rather than edited post-approval."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a027a233f179a78e8274ed5a276d93","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Run the pcb worker's pytest with pcb/worker/.venv/bin/python, not the ambient interpreter","created_at":"2026-08-22T04:02:16","updated_at":"2026-08-22T04:02:16","tags":["environment","pcb","pyclipper","pytest","testing","venv"],"value":"    cd pcb/worker && .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests -q --ignore=tests/oracle\n\nThe ambient interpreter (/home/imran/anaconda3) LACKS pyclipper; pcb/worker/.venv has pyclipper 1.4.0. Running with the ambient one produces a ~116-failure zone-fill cascade across test_zone_fill, test_coupon_board, test_gerbers, test_silk_projection, test_mask_projection, the gc7/gc9/gc10/gc11 checks, test_cutouts, test_ir_parity, test_determinism_gate and test_hermetic_check.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORTH A HINT rather than being obvious: the cascade looks exactly like a real regression. It is 116 confident-looking failures with plausible names, on a branch that touches DRC and geometry. During SR2FAB I ran it on the ambient interpreter, saw 116 failures on the BRANCH, ran the merge base, saw the same 116, and concluded \"pre-existing, not mine\" — a correct conclusion reached from a broken measurement. Codex diagnosed the real cause by running both worktrees under identical dependencies and getting main 2605 passed / branch 2650 passed, both green.\n\nTwo lessons beyond the command:\n- A baseline comparison protects you from misattributing failures, but NOT from a bad environment: both sides were equally wrong, so the diff looked clean.\n- tests/oracle additionally needs kicad-cli and is environment-sensitive on top of this; exclude it or expect one more failure.\n\nAlso non-hermetic, and NOT caused by any branch: `go test ./...`'s TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_DeclaredSchemaArgsOnly lets check_libraries consult the ambient Minerva plugin-data directory, so it fails when that lacks R_0805/C_0805/TH_TestPoint even though the repo's own pcb/library/footprints/ has them. `go test ./internal/...` is clean.\n\nRelated: [[pcb/test.baseline-before-branch-switch]]","component":"pcb","key":"test.use-the-worker-venv","confidence":"high","research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a028512e2671a2b2f98fdf95f705ed","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb: _arr_pair quantizes an internal polyline — dead, and a landmine for the boundary rule","description":"panel_tools._arr_pair applies _mm() to coordinates whose only consumer is _route_polyline -> rec[\"polyline\"], which is read for pts.size() / waypoint_count and nothing else. The raw segments remain the ingest/materialization geometry, so the quantization is DEAD: it changes no behaviour today.\n\nRaised by Codex at the SR2FAB approval (question 01a0274b0e9a comment 50) as non-blocking hygiene, and NOT fixed in that branch on purpose — the approval is pinned to 085e8cf and a post-approval edit, however small, is an unreviewed delta.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH DOING ANYWAY, and why this is filed rather than dropped: SR2FAB produced two regressions from exactly one confusion — applying the reply-boundary quantization rule to something that is not a reply. One crashed a validator by snapping a null sentinel (width_mm in _validate_route_intent); the other moved a collision body by 0.00005mm before a polygon intersection (_other_ghost_targets). A third site that quantizes an internal path and happens to be harmless is a standing invitation for the next reader to conclude the rule applies to internals. Dead code that models a wrong rule is worse than dead code.\n\nFIX: pass the pair through verbatim (float(), no _mm) and say at the site that the polyline is internal and size-only. Confirm _route_polyline's consumers still only read length; if a later change ever puts polyline coordinates in a reply, quantize THERE.\n\nRelated: the not-snapped boundary is documented at each deliberate site in panel_tools (model writes, %-formatted display values, the change journal, camera state).","created_at":"2026-08-22T07:13:24","updated_at":"2026-08-22T07:13:24","priority":3,"tags":["hygiene","pcb","quantization","sr2fab-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02873cad3747ba8cc279f5b98fbf0","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"GC7 reports phantom zone-clearance violations on any pour that declares no clearance_mm","created_at":"2026-08-22T07:51:12","updated_at":"2026-08-22T11:48:00","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["drc","false-positive","gc7","pcb","smart-remote-v2","zone-fill"],"environment":"Found during the SR2FAB operational replay on the live smart-remote-v2 board, plugin_build 0.2.0+sr2fab. PRE-EXISTING — neither gc7 nor the filler was touched by that epoch. It became visible only because SR2FAB's S2 made this board compile for the first time, so geometric DRC could run on it at all.</environment>\n<parameter name=\"repro_steps\">A board with a copper pour that does NOT author zone.clearance_mm, and foreign-net copper inside the pour region. smart-remote-v2 is the live instance: GND pour on bottom, SW_NODE trace + via_3 on bottom inside it.\n\nminerva_pcb_board_check reports verdict \"violations\", promotable false, refusals [\"geometric DRC reports 3 finding(s)\"], all three gc7_zone_clearance with measured_mm 0.0 and required_mm 0.2.</repro_steps>\n<parameter name=\"expected_result\">No findings. The pour IS correctly carved: the filler used 0.2 mm and there is no copper closer than 0.2 mm to anything.</expected_result>\n<parameter name=\"findings\">MEASURED, not inferred. I reproduced gc7's own intersection offline against the exported board and printed the overlap AREAS:\n\n  segment:6308a9fc...   18,103 nm^2   (0.000000018 mm^2)\n  segment:6f0add2b...    1,858 nm^2   (0.000000002 mm^2)\n  via:48cf7c55...       24,927 nm^2   (0.000000025 mm^2)\n\nFor scale, one square micrometre is 1,000,000 nm^2. The largest of these, squared off, is about 158 nm on a side. It is not copper; it is the difference between two polygonal approximations of the same arc.\n\nROOT CAUSE, and it is a coincidence of two correct decisions:\n1. zone_fill._clearance_mm: `floor = zone.clearance_mm`, and when that is None it falls back to board.design_rules.minimums.min_clearance_mm. This pour declares none, so it carves at 0.2.\n2. drc_geometric._check_gc7_zone_clearance: `required = _effective_min_clearance(global_min, ...)` = 0.2, with no net classes to raise it.\n\nGC7's docstring argues it is not circular because \"the FILLER carves by the ZONE's authored clearance_mm ... while this check uses _effective_min_clearance\". That is true when the zone AUTHORS a clearance. When it does not, both sides resolve to the identical number by design — zone_fill._clearance_mm's own docstring says \"Same rule resolution the geometric DRC applies, so the fill obeys the rule it will be judged against\". The two boundaries are then mathematically coincident.\n\n3. Both sides approximate round joins polygonally (PyclipperOffset(2.0, 5000), JT_ROUND) but through different call paths, so the approximations differ by sub-nanometre amounts and the intersection is a sliver rather than empty.\n\n4. GC7 has NO AREA GUARD: `overlap = clipper.Execute(...)` then `if not overlap: continue` and straight to a finding. Any non-empty result, however degenerate, is a violation.\n\nBLAST RADIUS: this is the DEFAULT configuration. A pour that authors no explicit clearance_mm is the common case, and any foreign copper inside it will trip this. It makes promote impossible on such a board, and the refusal names a clearance violation that does not exist.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE NUISANCE: it is a FALSE POSITIVE on the check that guards the largest piece of copper on the board. A reviewer who learns that gc7 cries wolf on every default pour is being trained to wave through the one time it is right.</findings>\n<parameter name=\"description\">SUGGESTED FIX, smallest first:\n\n(a) Add a minimum-area guard to gc7 — ignore an overlap whose total area is below a sliver threshold. The threshold has to be argued, not invented: it should be tied to the offset arc tolerance (5000 nm) rather than picked. An overlap that cannot represent real copper at the fabrication grid is not a violation. This is the narrow fix and it does not weaken the check: a genuine under-carve produces an overlap many orders of magnitude larger (a 0.1 mm under-carve on a 0.8 mm trace is ~10^5 mm^2 scale, not 10^-8).\n\n(b) Alternatively, have gc7 inflate by `required - EPS` so a boundary-coincident carve cannot intersect at all. Cheaper but it silently loosens the rule by EPS, which is worse on the honesty axis.\n\n(a) is preferred, with the threshold DERIVED and stated at the site.\n\nTESTS: the natural fixture is exactly this shape — a pour with no authored clearance_mm and a foreign trace inside it, asserting zero findings; plus a genuine under-carve (zone authoring a clearance BELOW the board minimum) asserting the finding still fires. The second is what stops the guard from being a blanket exemption. Both are cheap and neither exists today, which is why this shipped.\n\nNOT FIXED IN THE SR2FAB BRANCH. That branch is Codex-approved at 085e8cf and this is a pre-existing defect in code it did not touch; folding an unreviewed DRC change into an approved branch is the wrong trade. Filed for its own cycle.</description>\n"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02881647e7c6d9d1fc4dacaf6a167","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Most of the zone model is UNREACHABLE — clearance/thermal/priority have no MCP and no GUI path","created_at":"2026-08-22T08:06:03","updated_at":"2026-08-22T08:06:03","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["ergonomics","parity","pcb","smart-remote-v2","unreachable-property","zones"],"environment":"Found during the SR2FAB operational replay on the live smart-remote-v2 board, plugin_build 0.2.0+sr2fab. Independently verified by Fable against manifest.json, panel_tools.gd and PCBPanel.gd. Pre-existing; nothing in SR2FAB touched the zone surface.</environment>\n<parameter name=\"repro_steps\">Ask for the GND pour's clearance to be set to 0.25 on smart-remote-v2. There is no way to do it from either surface.\n\nAGENT: the eight zone verbs are create_zone, delete_zone, describe_zone, list_zones, propose_zone, set_zone_layer, set_zone_net, set_zone_outline. None accepts clearance_mm in its manifest.json input_schema. create_zone's parameters are {editor_name, kind, layer, net, outline}. _describe_zone returns exactly {zone_id, kind, net, layer, point_count, outline} (ui/panel_tools.gd:4366-4373). `grep clearance_mm ui/panel_tools.gd` returns zero zone hits.\n\nHUMAN: the zone property surface is _build_zone_rows (ui/PCBPanel.gd:1919-1968) — Kind as a read-only label, a Net dropdown, a Layer dropdown. Nothing else, and no dialog elsewhere (`grep clearance_mm ui/*.gd` hits only model files).</parameter>\n<parameter name=\"expected_result\">A property that the canonical schema documents (docs/board-yaml.md:189), that the model stores, and that decides both how the pour carves and whether GC7 passes should be settable by at least one of the two surfaces.</parameter>\n<parameter name=\"findings\">THE REACHABLE ZONE SURFACE IS: kind, net, layer, outline. That is all.\n\nUNREACHABLE, the whole tail of ResolvedZone (worker/pcb_worker/resolved_board.py:1043-1054):\n  clearance_mm      — decides the carve AND whether gc7 passes\n  min_thickness_mm\n  priority\n  connect_mode\n  thermal (thermal_gap_mm / thermal_bridge_width_mm)\n\nTHERMAL IS WORSE THAN UNREACHABLE. Authoring it makes zone_fill._refuse_thermal (zone_fill.py:838-845) refuse the fill outright — so the only way to reach that field is to hand-edit YAML, and doing so breaks the board. A property whose sole authoring path leads to a refusal is a trap.\n\nWHY clearance_mm IS URGENT RATHER THAN MERELY MISSING: it is the ONLY knob that breaks the gc7 coincident-boundary tie (bug 01a02873cad3). Measured on this board — raising board min_clearance_mm from 0.2 to 0.25 or 0.3 does NOT clear the phantom findings, because gc7's requirement rises with it; a net-class minimum does the same. Only a per-zone clearance that DIFFERS from the effective requirement works. So the documented workaround for a P1 that blocks promote is unreachable from both surfaces.\n\nONE ASTERISK ON THE READ SIDE: zones are held VERBATIM in the model (ui/model/pcb_data.gd:110-131), so minerva_pcb_export_yaml returns any authored clearance and an agent can READ it by parsing that document. There is no write path at all, from either surface.\n\nOWNER-PARITY NOTE: the owner is GUI-only. This is therefore not the usual \"MCP has it, the GUI does not\" parity gap — neither side has it, so no human instruction and no agent action can author the value. The only author is a text editor outside the application.</parameter>\n<parameter name=\"description\">SUGGESTED FIX, small and in two halves:\n\n1. AGENT: accept `clearance_mm` on minerva_pcb_create_zone, return it from minerva_pcb_describe_zone, and add minerva_pcb_set_zone_clearance mirroring set_zone_layer/set_zone_net (same no-op-on-unchanged, one-journalled-step, named-refusal shape). Decide deliberately whether to expose the rest of the tail; priority and min_thickness_mm are cheap, and connect_mode/thermal should stay closed until the pending v1 solid-vs-spokes owner ruling (accumulator R-d) is made, since authoring thermal today only produces a refusal.\n\n2. HUMAN: one more property row in _build_zone_rows (ui/PCBPanel.gd:1919-1968) beside Kind/Net/Layer. The owner is GUI-only, so this half is not optional — an MCP-only fix would leave the person who owns the board unable to set it.\n\nWHAT NOT TO DO, and this is why the workaround was refused during the replay: minerva_pcb_load_board with a hand-modified YAML reaches the field but is a DESTRUCTIVE whole-board replace. Its inline `yaml` form \"adopts nothing\" — annotations and the routing-workspace sidecar (ui/model/pcb_routing_sidecar.gd:145-235) are restored only on the `path` form with a sidecar beside the file — and undo/journal history is destroyed unconditionally. The delta honesty report covers entity counts, NOT session state. On a board whose only truth is the live editor plus drive sync, swapping the whole board to inject one field the app refuses to expose is a hazard, not a workaround.</parameter>\n</invoke>\n"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a029392ab57fb1966f4ab1a228b586","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb/test.mutation-testing-hygiene","created_at":"2026-08-22T11:26:47","updated_at":"2026-08-22T11:26:47","value":"Mutation-testing the worker: (1) COMMIT FIRST — a `git checkout pcb_worker/` restore helper silently wipes uncommitted source edits, and every row after the first then measures HEAD, not your work. (2) Disable bytecode caching: PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, `python -B`, `-p no:cacheprovider`, and rm -rf __pycache__ between rows. Editing a single-digit constant (GC7_SLIVER_WIDTH_NM = 2 -> 4) leaves the FILE SIZE UNCHANGED, so stale .pyc reuse gives a non-monotonic matrix (thresholds 2/3/5 \"failing\" while 10/50 \"passed\"). (3) grep-verify each mutation landed in the file before running. Both failure modes produced confident wrong matrices in one session.","component":"pcb","key":"test.mutation-testing-hygiene"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02b570e4d7072800fe51b70c45720","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Ratsnest as routing instruction: pad-scoped route target over MST/island airwires","description":"## Problem (owner, 2026-08-22, smart-remote v2)\n\n\"The ratsnest is too ratty — I can't tell what pins/pads want to connect.\" Then, more precisely: **\"What do I route this pin/pad to? I route pins one by one, and I need to know the two endpoints of the trace to figure out how to draw it.\"**\n\nThe second statement is the real requirement and it reframes the feature. The designer is not asking to browse a connectivity graph; they are asking for the other end of the trace they are about to draw.\n\n## Design direction\n\n**Core principle: do not make the designer interpret a graph while routing; turn the graph into one immediate routing instruction.**\n\nStarting a trace on a routable pad activates a transient, pad-scoped **route target**:\n\n- dim unrelated airwires; highlight the source copper island;\n- show exactly ONE prominent connection to the nearest unconnected island on that net;\n- mark and label the destination (refdes.pad, net, distance);\n- **lock that target for the duration of the routing gesture** — recompute only on commit or cancel;\n- for an offscreen destination, show an edge arrow plus \"frame both endpoints\", with NO automatic camera movement.\n\nComponent selection may still reveal all incident one-hop connections as an overview, but pad focus is the primary hand-routing interaction.\n\n## What this SUPERSEDES\n\nThis DCR was originally filed as *selection-scoped reveal* — select a component, its airwires brighten, the rest fade; pin-inspect a pad for the single-pad version anchored on the existing INSPECT_PIN tool. Two owner rulings changed it:\n\n1. **Pin inspection is rejected as the trigger** — \"not obvious; I wouldn't think to use pin inspection for this case.\" The routing gesture itself activates the focus. Depends on the trace tool already hit-testing pads; VERIFY before building (the earlier objection to pad scoping rested on pads not being selection entities in SELECT mode).\n2. **Connectivity-depth levels are dropped from the routing story.** Dimming other airwires plus endpoint highlighting \"removes the need for zoom levels.\" A degree definition was developed and is retained for the separate, unscheduled *comprehension* story — \"how does this section connect\" — which must not be conflated with routing. It lives in the child discussion, section 2.\n\nThe earlier rejection of a 0-3 depth knob with a HUD stepper, a source lock on `L`, and state in `pcb_set_preference` stands, on the original grounds: `ui/pcb_prefs.gd` is registry-gated and plugin-scoped so a per-board view anchor violates its charter (the real channel is `minerva_pcb_view_state` over the `_VIEW_FLAGS` family, currently bool-only); `L` is already lock-selected-components and `N` already toggles the ratsnest; and 2-3 hops answer no demonstrated routing question.\n\n## Why the renderer is the prerequisite\n\nFiltering the current renderer would only make arbitrary pairings less visually noisy. `_draw_ratsnest` chains pins in raw declaration order, subtracts nothing for copper already placed, and does not quiet power nets — so no amount of scoping makes it answer \"what do I route this to.\" **The MST edge incident to a pad IS the routing instruction**, and it only exists once islands are computed from real copper. See the child work item.\n\n## Build order (accepted verbatim from Codex, comment 55)\n\n1. Copper-island-aware MST renderer (child work item).\n2. Route-start pad focus with one locked destination.\n3. Correct the already-connected and pour-covered states.\n4. Offscreen target arrow and \"frame both endpoints\".\n5. Tap-anywhere / constrained-net metadata.\n6. Destination loupe or unrouted worklist — ONLY if testing shows remaining need.\n\n## Rules the implementation must honour\n\n- **Islands are physical**: built from pads, traces, vias and pours — not netlist membership.\n- **Exactly one suggestion.** No ranked list, no picker (owner ruling). Tie-break DETERMINISTIC (lowest refdes or coordinate), never random — a coin toss makes the same board answer differently between sessions.\n- **Ship named-pad targets first.** Mid-trace T-taps only for nets explicitly marked tap-anywhere; constrained nets (feedback/Kelvin, differential, high-speed, high-current) must require a named endpoint. Defaulting to pad-endpoints-only fails in the harmless direction. Rationale and the failure taxonomy are in the child discussion, section 4.\n- **A pad served by a pour is \"nothing to route\"**, not an airwire to a distant pad. A pad already joined by copper disappears entirely.\n- **Rails are dimmed, never silently hidden**, and labelled by UNRESOLVED count (\"GND · 27 unresolved\"), not net size.\n- **Do not rely on `is_power_net`** — measured False on every net of this board, including GND.\n- Do not add view state to `pcb_prefs`; use `view_state` if persistence is needed. Do not rebind `L` or `N`. Register any new draw-affecting state in `CAPTURE_MIRRORED_FIELDS` (`ui/pcb_canvas.gd`) or agent screenshots silently disagree with the screen.\n- The suggestion is **guidance, not enforcement**, unless a topology constraint exists on that net.\n\n## Deferred alternative (not chosen)\n\nUnrouted worklist panel — a sidebar listing inter-island airwires ordered by length or proximity, grouped by net. Reframes to \"what should I route next near here\" and is the only option that scales past what canvas overlays show. Now build-order step 6, gated on evidence.\n\n## Falsifier\n\nShip steps 1-3 and route a session by hand. If the designer still has to hunt for the other endpoint — or the single suggested target is wrong often enough that they want the list back — then one-answer-no-picker is the wrong ruling and the ranked set returns.\n\n## Status\n\nDesign agreed; no implementation started. Child work item (MST renderer) is the blocker and must land first.","created_at":"2026-08-22T21:18:40","updated_at":"2026-08-22T23:18:08","priority":2,"tags":["canvas","pcb","ratsnest","selection","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02b5763e87b84a9318b28f7437fa7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Ratsnest renderer draws a raw pin-list chain, not MST airwires","description":"## The mis-design\n\n`ui/pcb_canvas.gd:2629-2656` (`_draw_ratsnest`): for every net with >= 2 pins it draws a dashed chain in **raw pin-list order** — `pin[i] -> pin[i+1]` — at alpha 0.6 in the per-net colour. Three defects compound:\n\n1. **Not a spanning tree.** Chain order is the order pins happen to sit in the net's pin list, so lines criss-cross the board arbitrarily instead of connecting near neighbours.\n2. **No routed-pair subtraction.** Despite the \"unrouted net connections\" comment, pads already joined by committed copper still get airwires drawn between them. Airwires never shrink as routing lands.\n3. **No power-net quieting.** A 36-pin GND net contributes 35 lines, drawn in `Color.BLACK` (GND's default, `ui/pcb_net.gd:228`).\n\nNet effect on smart-remote v2: the ratsnest is unreadable, which is the owner-reported \"too ratty\" complaint. This is a renderer defect, not a missing filter feature — no amount of filtering fixes wrong geometry.\n\n## Intended behaviour\n\n- **Per-net Euclidean MST** instead of the chain: each unconnected copper island gets exactly one airwire to its nearest island.\n- **Island union**: union pads already joined by committed copper into islands and draw airwires only *between* islands, so lines disappear one by one as copper lands. The pad-snap tolerance logic in `PcbAnnotationHost.pin_info` (`:1013-1022`) is the seam to reuse for endpoint-to-pad association.\n- **Power-net quieting**: draw power-net airwires heavily dimmed (or nearest-neighbour only) unless a member is selected. `is_power_net` exists (`ui/pcb_net.gd:25`, contract `internal/board/minpcb.go:56`) but is not guaranteed set on agent-created nets — needs the name-heuristic fallback pattern already used by `generate_color_for_name:223`.\n\nThis is what KiCad does, i.e. the baseline the owner is unconsciously comparing against.\n\n## Why this is a work item, not a DCR\n\nIt corrects an existing mis-design in the renderer rather than proposing a new design direction (owner convention). The new-direction half — selection/pin-scoped reveal — is the parent DCR.\n\n## Cost and risk\n\nSmall-to-medium: one function rewrite plus union-find. The island endpoint-tolerance math is the only real risk (getting \"is this trace endpoint on this pad\" wrong either merges islands that aren't connected, hiding a needed airwire, or fails to merge, leaving a phantom one).\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Register any new draw-affecting state in `CAPTURE_MIRRORED_FIELDS` (`ui/pcb_canvas.gd:9735`) or agent screenshots silently disagree with the screen.\n- `N` stays bound to ratsnest toggle (`:4102`); do not rebind.\n\n## Verification\n\nSmart-remote v2 with its 36-pin GND / 20-pin +3V3 nets is the natural fixture: GND should read as short local hops rather than a black hairball, and airwires between C12.1 and its VIN neighbours should have vanished now that `trace_8`..`trace_11` are committed.\n\n## Status\n\nFiled for the record only — no work started.","created_at":"2026-08-22T21:19:02","updated_at":"2026-08-22T21:19:09","priority":2,"tags":["canvas","pcb","ratsnest","renderer","ux"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a02b570e4d7072800fe51b70c45720"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02ba3fd1e76c6816cabaa1f2408c0","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Degree, tap legality, and what silently breaks a board","description":"# 0. Decisions taken (owner, 2026-08-22)\n\n1. **Codex's UX story wins.** The primary interaction is a transient, pad-scoped **route target** activated by the routing gesture itself: dim unrelated airwires, highlight the source island, show exactly one prominent connection to the nearest unconnected island, label the destination.\n2. **Pin inspection is REJECTED as the trigger** — \"not obvious; I wouldn't think to use pin inspection for this\". Starting a trace activates the focus directly. (This overrides the earlier suggestion to anchor pad scope on the existing INSPECT_PIN tool; that suggestion rested on pads not being selection entities in SELECT mode, but the trace tool must already hit-test pads to start a trace at all — verify before building.)\n3. **Depth / connectivity-zoom levels are NOT NEEDED for routing.** Dimming other airwires plus endpoint highlighting does the job. Degree remains defined (section 2) and available for the separate *comprehension* story — \"how does this section connect\" — which is unscheduled and must not be conflated with routing.\n4. **MST/island renderer is the prerequisite for everything.** Filtering the current chain would only make arbitrary pairings less visually noisy.\n\nBuild order (from Codex, accepted): (1) copper-island-aware MST renderer; (2) route-start pad focus with one locked destination; (3) correct already-connected and pour-covered states; (4) offscreen target arrow and Frame Both; (5) tap-anywhere / constrained-net metadata; (6) destination loupe or unrouted worklist ONLY if testing shows remaining need.\n\n**Core principle**: do not make the designer interpret a graph while routing; turn the graph into one immediate routing instruction.\n\n# 1. Why this exists\n\nThe DCR asks how to make the ratsnest legible. The conversation behind it moved the question twice, and both moves change what should be built:\n\n- from \"filter the ratsnest\" to \"what is a degree, such that it survives power rails\";\n- from \"show connectivity\" to **\"what do I route THIS pad to, right now\"** — because routing happens one pad at a time, and the designer needs two concrete endpoints to draw a trace.\n\nThe second reframe is the load-bearing one: it makes the MST/island renderer (child work item) the primary feature rather than a cosmetic fix, because a correctly computed airwire IS the routing instruction.\n\n# 2. What is a degree\n\n*(Retained for the comprehension story, which is separate from routing — see decision 3.)*\n\n## The problem with the obvious definitions\n\n- **Shared-net adjacency** degenerates: GND and +3V3 touch nearly everything, so all parts are 1 degree apart and the measure carries no information.\n- **Copper adjacency** is empty on an unrouted board — which is exactly when the ratsnest is needed — and it changes whenever the router runs, so degree would be a property of the routing session rather than of the design.\n- **\"Power vs signal\"** is the wrong axis. The battery path J5 -> F1 -> Q1 -> U4 (nets VBAT -> VBAT_F -> VIN) is power but is SHAPED like a signal chain: series elements, low fanout, direction. The designer wants to walk it.\n\nThe axis that works is **distributes-to-many vs connects-a-few-in-series**.\n\n## Measured facts (Fable, against the live board YAML; 37 components, 29 nets)\n\n- **`is_power_net` is False on EVERY net of this board, including GND.** Not \"sometimes missing\" — empirically worthless as a gate. No definition may depend on it.\n- **Component fanout** (distinct components touching a net, NOT pin count) separates cleanly: GND 29, +3V3 18, then a hard gap to VIN 5, everything else <= 4. Any threshold in 6..17 gives an identical partition.\n- Pin count and component fanout differ (GND is 36 pins / 29 components); component fanout is the one that means \"distribution\".\n\n## The definition\n\n> Degree between two components is the length of the shortest chain of shared LOCAL nets between them, where every component is one node, every local net makes all components touching it mutually adjacent, and a net is local unless it touches more components than the rail threshold — in which case it is a distribution rail: shown, but never walked through.\n\nComputation: node = component; edge = for each local net, all its components pairwise adjacent at cost 1; **rails contribute ZERO edges**; undirected BFS; built from the netlist, never from copper. Rail test = component_fanout > T (default 8; auto-lowered to max(4, ceil(N/2)) below 16 components). Per-net override beats the auto test.\n\n## Why \"rails contribute zero edges\" rather than \"do not cross this net\"\n\nThis is the clause that neutralizes **decoupling capacitors**, which would otherwise silently reconnect the graph. C7 has one leg on VIN and one on GND. Refusing to *cross* GND is not enough if the component still conducts between its nets. Because a rail contributes no adjacency at all, C7 has no onward edge to offer and is a leaf by construction — the walk enters and stops. No special-casing of capacitors.\n\nTwo independent derivations converged on this rule:\n- structural (component-node BFS, rails contribute no edges);\n- the owner's **breadboard model** — a degree is one jumper wire you must place; the rails are pre-wired strips you *terminate on but never transit through*. Every part is one wire from the rail, and that wire is termination, not transit.\n\nThe breadboard model also cleanly separates two questions that were being conflated: **what a rail MEANS** (terminate-only — structural, fixed) from **how a rail is DETECTED** (fanout, flag, or explicit override — swappable). A wrong detector then degrades the view instead of corrupting the definition.\n\n## Worked results\n\n- **Synthetic line** (36 parts daisy-chained, all sharing GND and +3V3): U1 to U36 = **35**. Matches designer intuition exactly.\n- **This board**: J5 -> F1 -> Q1 -> U4 walks as degrees **1, 2, 3**. GND is never hopped. C7 appears in the VIN shell then terminates. R1 is a hop (U1S -> R1 = 1, U1S -> U2 = 2).\n\n## Resolved sub-questions\n\n- **Series passives are a hop, not transparent.** Every component is a node uniformly. Transparency would need part-type metadata this board has already proven absent, and a series capacitor is transparent to AC but open to DC with no structural test to tell them apart. Offer transparency as a view toggle, never as the definition.\n- **Multi-pin nets**: all members mutually 1. A net is one equipotential; distance through it is 1 regardless of pin count.\n- **Undirected.** Pin-direction metadata is less reliable than the flag that measured all-False; buses are bidirectional; the line example yields 35 either way.\n- **Unrouted boards**: fully defined, because degree is a function of the netlist. Identical before and after routing.\n- **Connectors pass through by default** (enter J2 on I2C, leave via its +3V3_SW pin). NOTE: this is contested — component-as-node treats every multi-pin part as internally bonded, which is electrically false for all of them; it is right as a model of FUNCTIONAL flow, and connectors are where functional flow and electrical reality diverge most. A \"connectors terminate walks\" toggle exists; whether it defaults ON is open.\n\n## Known failure modes of the definition\n\n- **Mid-fanout legitimate signals.** A clock/reset/enable tree touching 9+ components auto-classifies as a rail and truncates the walk. The honest insight: fanout measures *distribution shape*, not rail-ness — a reset tree is distribution-shaped signal exactly as VBAT_F is chain-shaped power. The heuristic has the right axis and is a lossy measurement of it. Mitigation is UI, not a cleverer threshold: never drop a net silently; render it grayed with its unresolved count and a one-click override.\n- **Hub components.** U1S touches 20 of 29 nets, so selecting almost anything puts it at degree 1-2, and from U1S half the board is degree 1. Truthful, but visually it is the hairball this feature exists to kill. The shell view must survive a 15-part shell.\n- **All-rail parts.** A decoupler with both legs on rails is degree infinity from everything. Word it \"rail-only part\", never \"unconnected\", or it reads as a DRC error.\n- **Tiny boards.** Below ~16 components the relative threshold is least trustworthy; overrides matter most there.\n\n**Falsifier for the whole structural approach**: a board in the corpus where must-walk nets and must-block rails OVERLAP in component fanout — a clock tree touching more components than a local supply rail on the same board — so no threshold separates them. Here the gap is 5 vs 18. If that gap closes elsewhere, rail-ness must come from curated per-net metadata and this becomes a different product.\n\n# 3. The routing primitive — THE PRIMARY FEATURE\n\nRouting happens one pad at a time, so the operative question is not \"show me connectivity\" but **\"what is the other endpoint of the trace I am about to draw?\"**\n\n## What the interaction does\n\nTriggered by **starting a trace** on a routable pad (decision 2):\n\n- dim unrelated airwires;\n- highlight the source copper island;\n- show exactly ONE prominent connection to the nearest unconnected island on that net;\n- mark and label the destination — refdes.pad, net, distance;\n- **LOCK that target for the duration of the routing gesture** so it does not jump as the cursor moves; recompute only after commit or cancel;\n- for an **offscreen destination**, show an edge arrow plus a \"frame both endpoints\" affordance — and do NOT move the camera automatically.\n\nComponent selection may still reveal all incident one-hop connections as an overview, but **pad focus is the primary hand-routing UX**.\n\nThe target lock and the offscreen handling both come from Codex and are load-bearing: an unlocked target flickers mid-gesture and makes the feature worse than none, and at routing zoom on a board this size the destination is usually offscreen.\n\n## Consequences for the computation\n\n- **Degrees above 1 are comprehension, not routing.** You never route to something three hops away. Keep the two features distinct.\n- **The destination is an ISLAND, not a pad.** For a multi-pin net the answer is never \"the other 35 pads\"; it is the nearest copper on this net not already joined to you. The MST edge incident to the pad IS the routing instruction. This is why the current renderer cannot answer it: its pairs come from pin declaration order, so they are arbitrary rather than nearest.\n- **Islands are built from pads, traces, vias AND pours** — physical connectivity, not netlist membership.\n- **The destination may be a POINT on that island, often mid-trace.** The owner's model: once U1.1 -> U2.1 is routed, that trace becomes a \"wide pin component\", and U3.1 connects to a virtual point on it. Geometrically this is point-to-segment nearest-point (a T-junction tap), not point-to-point. **Ship named-pad targets FIRST; mid-trace taps only for nets marked tap-anywhere** (see section 4).\n- **Airwires re-aim as copper lands.** After U1-U2 exists, U3.1's target becomes the nearest point on that new trace — likely closer and in a different direction.\n- **Exactly ONE suggestion. Owner ruling: no ranked list, no picker.**\n- **Tie-break must be DETERMINISTIC, not a coin toss.** Lowest refdes or lowest coordinate. A coin toss makes the same board answer differently between sessions, and the view flickers for no actionable reason.\n- **Two states the answer must get right or it becomes untrustworthy**: a pad served by a pour is \"nothing to route — the plane covers this\", not an airwire to a distant pad; and a pad already joined by copper to its net disappears entirely rather than showing a line already drawn.\n- **The suggestion is guidance, not enforcement** — unless a topology constraint exists on that net.\n\n## Rails in the resting view\n\nDim or collapse high-component-fanout distribution nets, but **never silently hide them**. Label with the UNRESOLVED count, not the net size — \"GND · 27 unresolved\" tells the designer work remaining; \"GND · 29 parts\" tells them nothing actionable. Selecting one of their pads must still reveal its single next instruction.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nThe accretion process the owner described is Prim's algorithm: grow one island, always take the nearest thing not yet joined. This exposes a fork worth choosing deliberately:\n\n- taking the **globally** nearest pair minimizes total copper;\n- taking the nearest thing **to where you are already working** minimizes hand movement and avoids jumping across the board between traces.\n\nThese give different orders and different totals. Hand routing wants the second. Recommendation: default next-suggestion to locality; let total length lose.\n\n# 4. When is a T-tap bad\n\n**The core answer: geometric DRC passes all of these.** DRC checks clearance, width, annular ring, hole-to-copper. Every failure mode below is a TOPOLOGY failure. The board fabricates perfectly and behaves wrong. (Evidence: this board's geometric DRC returns clean while 24 nets are entirely unrouted — that is what it is and is not measuring.)\n\n1. **Sense / feedback lines.** Must connect at one specific point; tapping anywhere convenient silently regulates the wrong node. **Live on this board**: U4.1 sits on +3V3 and on a fixed-output buck that pin is feedback sense, not the power outlet (READ FROM THE NETLIST, NOT THE DATASHEET — confirm against the AP63203 datasheet before relying on it). A tool suggesting a tap into the nearest +3V3 copper near the inductor would sense the wrong point and lose regulation under load. Nothing objects until the bench.\n2. **Stubs on fast edges.** A tap leaves a stub; a stub reflects once it is a meaningful fraction of the edge rate's wavelength. Requires rise-time knowledge, which is not in the netlist. Nothing on this board is fast enough to care.\n3. **Shared current paths.** Tap a branch off a power trace and the shared segment carries the sum of both loads. Requires current expectations, also not in the netlist.\n4. **Matched or paired nets.** Tapping one leg of a differential pair breaks the match. Detectable only if the tool knows they are a pair.\n\n## Consequence for the tool: a THIRD net category\n\n- **Rails** — terminate on, never transit; tap anywhere; usually a pour.\n- **Ordinary signals** — tap anywhere.\n- **Constrained nets** — topology carries meaning (feedback/Kelvin, differential, high-speed, high-current). The tool must REFUSE to suggest a mid-trace tap and insist the endpoint be a named pad; it may also forbid branching entirely.\n\nThis classification cannot come from fanout or any structural test; it is design intent. But the tool need not guess it to be safe: **defaulting to pad-endpoints-only for any net not explicitly marked tap-anywhere fails in the harmless direction**, and pours already cover the case where tapping matters most. This is also why named-pad targets ship before mid-trace taps.\n\n## How a designer would actually find out\n\n- **Before** — encode intent as a per-net constraint (\"Kelvin, connect only at C10\"; \"max stub 5mm\") and check it. The gap is that nobody authors these; they live in datasheets and in heads.\n- **After** — the scope or the bench. Expensive.\n- **Practical middle** — the datasheet's own layout-guidance section. Every buck datasheet has one; it states where FB connects and how tight the input loop must be. Closest thing to an oracle available today.\n\n# 5. Other failure classes that pass DRC and still break a board\n\nRaised by the agent, not by the owner. All are invisible to geometric DRC.\n\n1. **No reference plane / undefined return path.** The GND pour on this board was deleted (deliberately). Every high-speed or switching signal's return current then takes an arbitrary path, raising loop area and EMI. On a 2-layer board the bottom pour normally IS the return path; routing signals as though a plane exists when it does not is a silent, board-wide defect.\n2. **Return-path discontinuity.** Even with a plane, routing a signal across a split or a slot in the reference forces the return current around it. Classic EMI failure; DRC is silent.\n3. **Shared ground between analog and digital.** This board has an audio amp (U2) and a mic/codec (U3) alongside a switching regulator. Amp return currents through shared ground inject noise into the mic path. A topology question (star vs daisy vs partitioned plane), invisible to any geometric check.\n4. **Buck input loop area.** Cin -> VIN -> SW -> GND is the high-di/dt loop; its physical area is what radiates. C7 (VIN) and C12 (VIN bulk) must sit tight against U4's VIN/GND pins. Placement-and-routing coupled, checked by nothing.\n5. **Trace width vs current.** Geometric DRC checks a MINIMUM width, never whether width suits the current. See section 6 — this is live on this board right now.\n6. **Via current capacity.** A single via carries far less current than the trace feeding it. Power nets crossing layers need multiple stitched vias; one via is a thermal and reliability defect that passes every check.\n7. **Antenna keepout.** U1S is an ESP32-class module with an integrated antenna. Copper under or near the antenna detunes it and kills range. This board carries two keepout zones (top and bottom, 4 points each) which are plausibly for exactly this — routing through them fabricates fine and produces a radio that does not work.\n8. **Thermal relief vs solid pad connection.** Solid connections to a plane wick heat during hand soldering and cause cold joints; thermal reliefs restrict current. Wrong choice either fails assembly or overheats in service.\n9. **Floating CMOS inputs.** An unconnected high-impedance input oscillates and draws current. Nets exist for pins that are NC by design; nothing distinguishes \"intentionally NC\" from \"forgotten\".\n10. **Via-in-pad without fill.** Solder wicks down the via during reflow, starving the joint.\n11. **Acid traps / acute copper angles.** Manufacturing defect class; some fabs check, this DRC does not.\n12. **Mounting-hole clearance.** Screw heads and standoffs short nearby copper. A mechanical constraint expressed nowhere in the netlist.\n\n# 6. Live finding on this board (needs its own item)\n\nThe four VIN traces committed during this session landed at **0.25 mm** (`width_source: board_rules`), while every pre-existing VIN trace on the board is **0.8 mm** (measured via `minerva_pcb_export_trace_geometry`). VIN is the input path to a 2 A buck. A 0.25 mm input trace is plausibly undersized and is certainly inconsistent with the rest of the net.\n\nSeparately, that same export now reports 18 traces with only ONE of the four committed traces present. The commit reply reported four trace ids (trace_8..trace_11). The discrepancy is unexplained and must be diagnosed before anything else is committed to this board — do not treat the commit report as ground truth.\n\n# 7. Open questions\n\n1. Does the \"connectors terminate walks\" toggle default ON or OFF? *(Comprehension story only — does not block routing work.)*\n2. What does the shell view do about hub components (U1S at degree 1 from half the board)? *(Comprehension story only.)*\n3. Does the rail threshold survive a second board, or do the fanout ranges overlap there?\n4. Who authors the constrained-net list, and where does it live so both the panel and agents read one copy?\n5. Does the trace tool already hit-test pads (needed for decision 2)? Verify before building step 2.","created_at":"2026-08-22T22:42:42","updated_at":"2026-08-22T23:17:08","tags":["degree","design-intent","pcb","ratsnest","routing","ux"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a02b570e4d7072800fe51b70c45720"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02bc4f8007e0daa1f3a65267ab3c1","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Proposed routes take the board default width, ignoring the net's established width","description":"WHAT IS WRONG\nA route proposed without an explicit width takes the board default rather than the width already established on that net, producing a trace far narrower than the copper it joins. On a power net this is a current-carrying defect that no gate catches.\n\nWHERE\n`minerva_pcb_workspace_propose` / `workspace_commit`; the candidate records carry `width_source: \"board_rules\"` when no width is supplied on the span. Measured on smart-remote v2 via `minerva_pcb_export_trace_geometry`: routes proposed onto VIN committed at **0.25 mm**, while every pre-existing VIN trace on that board is **0.8 mm**. VBAT/VBAT_F are 1.0 mm, BST is 0.4 mm — so the board clearly carries per-net width intent that the propose path does not read.\n\nCONSEQUENCE\nSilent and physical. VIN is the input path to a 2 A buck (U4, AP63203); a 0.25 mm input trace is plausibly undersized for it. Geometric DRC passes because GC1 checks a MINIMUM width, never whether the width suits the current — so nothing objects at propose, commit, DRC or fabrication. The board is manufacturable and wrong. This is failure class 5 in the child discussion of DCR 01a02b570e4d (\"trace width vs current\").\n\nThe second-order harm is worse than the first: an agent proposing routes onto power nets will keep producing undersized copper, and each instance looks identical to a correct one in every report the tool emits.\n\nACCEPTANCE\nA route proposed onto a net that already carries copper adopts that net's established width, or refuses and says why. The observable: propose a span on a net whose existing traces are 0.8 mm with no width argument, and the candidate reports 0.8 mm — not 0.25 mm with `width_source: board_rules`.\n\nThe criterion a lazy fix fails: simply raising the board default would make this span correct and every thin-signal-net span wrong. The width must come from the NET, not from a different global.\n\nTRAPS\n- A net with mixed existing widths has no single answer; decide whether that is \"widest wins\", \"nearest segment wins\", or a refusal, and say which.\n- A net with no existing copper has nothing to inherit — the board default is genuinely correct there, so the fix must not make a fresh net an error.\n- `width_source` already exists as a field and reports honestly; the defect is which source is consulted, not the reporting.\n\nLINKS\nSurfaced while dispatching DCR 01a02b570e4d. Related failure taxonomy: discussion 01a02ba3fd1e section 5.","created_at":"2026-08-22T23:18:43","updated_at":"2026-08-23T00:16:14","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["pcb","power","routing","silent-defect","width"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02bc53fcf71b08a1dfca90db0d807","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"NOT A DEFECT — workspace_commit reported four trace ids correctly; the copper was deleted by hand four minutes later","description":"## Verdict\n\n**`minerva_pcb_workspace_commit` behaved correctly.** All four traces were written to the board. The export disagreed because the copper was removed by hand in the UI between the two measurements. The defect was in the method that filed this bug, not in the tool.\n\n## Evidence\n\n`minerva_pcb_get_change_journal` on editor \"Smart Remote v2\" (27 entries, the board's full forward log) reconciles the two surfaces exactly:\n\n```\n13:49:00  add_trace    VIN  trace_8   (2 segments)\n13:49:00  add_trace    VIN  trace_9   (2 segments)\n13:49:00  add_trace    VIN  trace_10  (2 segments)\n13:49:00  add_trace    VIN  trace_11  (4 segments)\n          ... 4 minutes ...\n13:53:00  remove_trace VIN  trace_8\n13:53:03  remove_trace VIN  trace_10\n13:53:05  remove_trace VIN  trace_9\n13:53:07  remove_trace VIN  trace_11\n13:53:12  rotate_component  C12  90° -> 0°\n13:53:14  move_component    C12  (66.0,62.5) -> (65.5,64.5)\n13:53:23  add_trace    VIN  trace:b015f142...  (1 segment)\n13:53:28  move_component    C12  -> (65.0,63.5)\n13:53:29  move_trace   trace:b015f142...  +0.05mm\n13:53:31  remove_trace trace:b015f142...\n   ... two more hand-drawn/adjusted/discarded single-segment traces ...\n13:54:28  add_trace    VIN  trace:ea3544a9...  (1 segment)  <- the only VIN 0.25mm\n                                                              trace in the export\n```\n\nThe four `add_trace` entries carry the exact segment counts of the four committed candidates (cand_3=2, cand_5=2, cand_7=2, cand_9=4). The commit reply's ids and counts were accurate at the moment it returned.\n\nThe removals are interleaved with `rotate_component`, `move_component`, and `move_trace` nudges of 0.05 mm, spaced 2-3.5 seconds apart. That is an interactive editing session — a person deleting the four routes, re-orienting C12, and hand-drawing replacements — not a tool path.\n\nCode review agrees. In `RoutingWorkspace._commit_batch` (`ui/model/pcb_routing_workspace.gd:2375-2410`) each `board.add_trace` is followed by a `board.get_trace(tid) == null` check that rolls the whole batch back if the board accepted a trace and then could not find it. A commit that returns `ok` has already proved every trace is present.\n\n## Where the original report was also wrong\n\nIt claimed the surviving 0.25 mm VIN trace \"matches candidate cand_3\". It does not. cand_3 is a two-segment L — (66.0,63.975) -> (70.5,63.975) -> (70.5,63.45). The surviving trace `trace:ea3544a9...` is a single segment (70.5,63.45) -> (66.475,63.4787), drawn by hand at 13:54:28. **No committed candidate's geometry survives on the board.** The \"one of four survived\" framing was a coincidence of net, width and rough position — not an identification.\n\n## The real lesson\n\nTwo surfaces disagreed and I reached for a defect in the one that reported success, without consulting the append-only log that records what happened between them. The change journal was available the whole time and answers the question in one call. A discrepancy between two readings taken minutes apart on a board a human is actively editing is not evidence of a lying tool.\n\nSeverity 1 was assigned on the reasoning that \"a commit reply is the evidence an agent uses to report this landed.\" That reasoning was sound; the premise it rested on was unmeasured.\n\n## What this DOES corroborate\n\nThe four deleted traces were 0.25 mm on VIN — the exact defect in bug 01a02bc4f800. The owner deleted all four within four minutes and hand-routed a replacement. That is independent confirmation that the width defect produces copper a designer rejects on sight.\n\n## What it left behind\n\nOne real defect, filed separately: deleting committed copper leaves the routing workspace believing it is still routed. The four candidates still report `disposition: committed` with `committed_trace_ids: [trace_8..trace_11]`, and their tasks still report `closed`, though none of that copper exists.\n\n## Consequence for the board\n\nNo new commits were blocked on the wrong grounds for long, but the standing instruction \"do not commit anything else to that board until explained\" is **lifted** — the board is in the state the owner hand-edited it into, and the tool is trustworthy.","created_at":"2026-08-22T23:19:02","updated_at":"2026-08-23T00:16:06","priority":1,"severity":4,"tags":["commit","diagnosed","method-failure","not-a-defect","pcb","routing"],"resolution":"Not a defect. The change journal shows all four traces were written by the commit and removed by hand in the UI four minutes later, interleaved with a C12 rotate/move and hand-drawn replacements. The commit reply was accurate; the export was taken after unrelated interactive edits. Real residue filed separately as the workspace-staleness bug."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02bf972247712b3f472fde5dbd52f","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Deleting committed copper leaves the routing workspace believing the span is still routed","description":"## What is wrong\n\nRemoving a trace from the board does not tell the routing workspace. A candidate whose committed copper has been deleted keeps `disposition: committed`, keeps `committed_trace_ids` naming trace ids that no longer exist, and its routing task stays `closed`. The workspace reports the span as routed when there is no copper.\n\n## Measured\n\nOn editor \"Smart Remote v2\", `minerva_pcb_workspace_list include_terminal:true net:VIN` currently returns:\n\n- cand_3 — `disposition: committed`, `committed_trace_ids: [\"trace_8\"]`, task `VIN|ann_0021` state `closed`\n- cand_5 — `committed_trace_ids: [\"trace_9\"]`, task closed\n- cand_7 — `committed_trace_ids: [\"trace_10\"]`, task closed\n- cand_9 — `committed_trace_ids: [\"trace_11\"]`, task closed\n\n`minerva_pcb_export_trace_geometry` on the same editor returns 18 traces and **none of trace_8..trace_11 is among them**. The change journal shows all four removed at 13:53 on 2026-08-22.\n\nThe `validation` field did go `stale` on all four — but that reports the DRC verdict's freshness, not whether the copper exists. Nothing distinguishes \"this route is committed and on the board\" from \"this route is committed and someone deleted it.\"\n\n## Where\n\n`PCBData.remove_trace` (`ui/model/pcb_data.gd:819`) erases the trace and emits `trace_changed` / `data_changed`. It does not notify the bound routing workspace. `PCBData.bind_routing_workspace` (`:2487`) exists but its only use is workspace snapshotting for undo.\n\n`RoutingWorkspace.uncommit` (`ui/model/pcb_routing_workspace.gd:1424`) is the function that would repair this — it reverts the candidate to its prior disposition, clears the copper ids, and reopens the task, with a docstring saying \"the copper is gone → the task reopens\". **It has no caller.** Grep of `ui/` and `tests/gd/` finds only its definition and comments describing when it ought to fire. Undo does not call it either: undo restores the whole workspace layer from the snapshot attached at commit (`_restore_workspace_snapshot`, `pcb_data.gd:2813`), which is a coherent design and explains why the gap went unnoticed — the undo path is covered, the plain-deletion path is not.\n\n## Consequence\n\nQuiet and cumulative. The workspace is the surface that answers \"what still needs routing\", and the DCR 01a02b570e4d ratsnest work is going to lean on exactly that answer. A closed task over deleted copper is an unrouted span that no surface reports as unrouted. It fails in the reassuring direction: the board looks more finished than it is.\n\nIt also mints unreachable state. A candidate stuck at `committed` cannot be re-committed — `_commit_batch` refuses with \"already committed — its copper is on the board; undo the commit (or uncommit it) before committing again\" (`:2167`), and since nothing calls `uncommit`, there is no way to take that advice. The span cannot be re-routed through its own candidate.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nDeleting a trace that a committed candidate owns reopens that candidate's task and stops the workspace reporting the span as routed. The observable: commit a candidate, delete its trace, then `workspace_list` — the task must not still read `closed` with `committed_trace_ids` naming a trace the export cannot find.\n\nThe criterion a lazy fix fails: filtering dead ids out of `committed_trace_ids` at read time would make the listing look consistent while leaving the task closed and the candidate unre-committable. The task state is the part that matters.\n\n## Traps\n\n- Undo already works via snapshot restore. A fix that also fires on undo would double-apply — establish which path is running before compensating.\n- Partial deletion is a real case: a candidate can own several traces and lose only one. Decide whether that reopens the task or reports a partial state, and say which.\n- `uncommit` may be the right seam or may be the wrong shape for this; it was written for a caller that never arrived. Do not assume calling it from `remove_trace` is correct without checking its reentrancy guard against the batch-commit transaction flag.\n\n## Links\n\nSurfaced by the investigation of bug 01a02bc53fcf, which turned out to be no defect at all — this is its only real residue. Relevant to DCR 01a02b570e4d, whose renderer work depends on an honest answer to \"what is still unrouted\".","created_at":"2026-08-23T00:16:03","updated_at":"2026-08-23T00:16:03","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["pcb","routing","staleness","trust","workspace"]}
